WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.669 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You have media
sources that you thought

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.105
were these pristine,
amazing institutions,

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:06.806
like the New York Times
or the Washington Post

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:10.677
or CNN or MSNBC, but
every single day and
every single night

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:15.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're running more articles
or running more stories

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:18.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
saying that, oh, the
breaking gun, breaking news.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:20.387
- [Dave] The walls
are crashing in.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The walls are crashing in,
and I'd see it every day.

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:26.359
I'd turn on Rachel Maddow
at night and I was like,

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
holy cow, do people
actually believe this?

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.096
And I write actually, you
know, Schumer used to be

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.465 align:start size:97% position:3%
a close friend of our family,
and he was going around

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.901
the Upper East Side telling
all my parents' friends,

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
Jared's about to be indicted,
he's gonna go to jail,

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.538
we know it for sure,
we have all the facts,

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:39.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
what they did to
collude with Russia.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.908
They were basically
denying an election.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:44.444
(gentle music)

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.683
- I'm Dave Rubin, and joining me

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:54.554
is a former senior adviser
to President Donald J. Trump

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.023
and author of the
brand new book,

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
the New York Times' number
one best selling book.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:03.496 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're gonna have to talk
about that in just a second.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.731
"Breaking History".

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.466 align:start size:84% position:16%
Jared Kushner, good
to see you, my friend.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:07.300
How's it going?

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.468
- Doing great.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:09.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
Thank you for
having me on, David.

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I am glad to be
sitting here with you.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:14.841
First off, they put
you at number one in
the New York Times.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.976
I don't think the New York
Times likes you very much,

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.411
and they did it.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
How does that feel,
because they didn't put me

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:20.980
on there at all
for my last book.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.384
So this is a little
bit of a tug here.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:28.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, it is funny
that it's given this height

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.723
as a goal to attain, but
I think that is the goal

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
that all the people who
are authors try to attain,

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:34.227
so it's nice to
have achieved it.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:38.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you try not to give
it too much value as well.

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.632
But it was kind of funny.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:43.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
They wrote a review
that was pretty unhinged

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:46.139
in terms of how they
characterized the book.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.574
And actually once that happened,

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:50.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
the sales of the book
started going up immediately,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think it's because
what's in the book

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.714
contradicts a lot of what
they wrote about Trump

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
and his administration
for four years.

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.885
And so they obviously didn't
want to see it in existence

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.720 align:start size:84% position:16%
or want people to
read a point of view

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.889
that differs from theirs.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
- They are not big fans
of that, that is for sure.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:07.894
I do appreciate the fact
that your book matches

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.396
our Locals set pretty perfectly.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.198
As you know, I just
got back on the grid.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.867
So you released,
the book came out

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.535
the last week of August, right?

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:17.871
So I did not get to
see any of your press

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.806
around this or anything.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.841
So I'm going into
this pretty blind,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.309 align:start size:88% position:13%
and when I've mentioned
you on the show,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.211 align:start size:97% position:3%
we've gotten to know each
other a little bit personally

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
over the last couple of months
since I moved down here.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.485
When I mention you on
the show, I often say

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.920
people don't know what
his voice sounds like.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.121
They see him, but
they don't know

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.689
what his voice sounds like.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
So let's do the
beginning stuff first.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.693
I'm a Long Island guy,
you're a Jersey guy.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:44.130 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had a similar background,
somewhat similar ages.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.933 align:start size:88% position:13%
Tell people a little
bit of the Jared Kushner

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.468
growing up story.

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:50.136
- I thought you were gonna
ask me to sing or something

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:51.638
with that build up.

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:54.807 align:start position:0%
So no, it's great, and obviously
it's been great having you

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:59.078 align:start position:0%
and your family down in Florida
now, your extended family.

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:01.848
So basically I grew
up in New Jersey

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
and had a lot of
different experiences.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.219 align:start size:97% position:3%
I try to write it very
quickly in the book, right?

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:09.656 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's like doing your
obituary in 5,000 pages,

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:10.857
trying to just condense it.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.493
But basically grew
up in a great suburb,

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:17.463 align:start size:97% position:3%
had amazing parents, and
just write about the journey.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:21.301 align:start size:97% position:3%
My life, I write about
obviously starting businesses

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.470 align:start size:88% position:13%
in college and whatnot,
and I write about

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.208
how afterwards I was in law
school and business school.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.545
Sorry, I'm not used to
nice questions like that.

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.381
- You're freaking out because
I'm not trying to get you.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.816
I'll get you in a second.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:39.519 align:start position:0%
- I'm used to the killers
coming at me, so it's too nice.

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:44.057 align:start size:97% position:3%
But basically I write in the
book about my upbringing.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.226
I was in law school at
NYU and business school

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:48.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
after graduating Harvard,
and I was working

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the Manhattan District
Attorney's office.

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.031
I thought I'd be a prosecutor.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.034
And then my father got
arrested and I realized

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.336
I didn't want to be
a prosecutor anymore.

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:59.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
I kind of saw what
happens when prosecutors

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
have a lot of political
ambition and unchecked power,

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:05.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I didn't think I
could be in that position.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.615 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I write through what
that experience was like,

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:10.116
how it changed my
view of the world.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:12.752 align:start size:88% position:13%
I met a lot of people
through that experience.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.054 align:start size:81% position:19%
I went into my
father's business.

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.223
It was a real time of problems.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:20.326 align:start size:97% position:3%
Really through that period,
that three year period

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then the three year period
of the financial downturn,

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.464 align:start size:88% position:13%
that was where I
learned a lot of lessons

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:27.800
that really taught me a lot.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.903
And then I write about
how I was very fortunate

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
when I was in New York to
meet a great beautiful woman

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:37.243
named Ivanka, who we had
an interesting courtship.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.112
She said yes, which
was really great.

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:40.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
I got the permission
from her dad.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.750 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you go to the
dad first on that one?

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I actually give a
little bit of this story,

00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:51.157
but I didn't really spend
too much time with him

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:53.259
for about the first year that
we were dating on and off.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:56.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then we were getting
serious, and so Ivanka said,

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:58.831
you know, my dad, you've
got to go talk to him,

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.433
because if not, he's
gonna start thinking

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:01.934
you're not interested.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.837
And so I reach out to him
and I say, hi Mr. Trump.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:05.905
This is Jared Kushner.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.407
I'm dating Ivanka.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.542
I'd really like to come over
and spend some time together.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.644
I didn't know what the
hell I was gonna say.

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:13.780
And he says, that's great, why
don't we have lunch tomorrow?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.349
I'm like, oh man, that's tough.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:19.018 align:start size:88% position:13%
I go over to Trump
Tower, and we have lunch

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.353
in the Trump Grill, and
what was funny about him

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:21.788
was he would never
go out for lunch.

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:23.656 align:start size:97% position:3%
So Ivanka was very surprised,
because he would always

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.391
have lunch by his desk.

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.327
He didn't like to waste
the time of going out.

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:29.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we go in Trump
Grill in Trump Tower,

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
he's got Trump
security guards around.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:33.466
- Trump napkins, Trump glasses.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.401
- Trump order, Trump
meatloaf, everything.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.371
And I said, look, Ivanka and I,

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:40.139
we're getting more serious now,

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:44.010
and she started a process
of converting to Judaism.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.345
He turns to me and says, well,

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:46.813
why does she have to
convert to Judaism?

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:48.314
Why can't you convert?

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:50.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I said actually, that's
a really good question.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:52.318
Nobody's asked me that question.

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:53.553 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you know, we've
been studying,

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:54.821
and this is how we
feel we want to go.

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.289
And he says, that's great.

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:59.292
You know, I've been in New
York real estate all my life.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:02.061
Everyone thinks I'm Jewish,
all my friends are Jewish.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.330
And from that point,
he was very supportive.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then he goes into another
thing which was funny,

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:09.669
that I tell in the
book, where he says,

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.903
as you know, Ivanka right now,

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:12.538
she's at an amazing
place in her life.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.575 align:start size:97% position:3%
A lot of my friends call,
they all respect her, he says,

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:16.909 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you know, Tom
Brady's a good friend,

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.678
and he's been dying
to take her out.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:20.580
And so I turned to
him and I said, well,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.615
if I was her I'd go
out with Tom Brady.

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:26.753
And he looks at me,
maybe getting my self
deprecating humor,

00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:28.788
or maybe it was serious,
and he basically looks

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:30.223
and says, yeah, I know.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:32.759
So it was good.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:35.094
- Wow, so you beat out
Tom Brady for Ivanka.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.596
- In his version of events.

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:38.030
Yeah, I don't know.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's close enough to
something that sounds right.

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:41.768
Can you just back
up a little bit

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
and explain a little bit
more about some of the stuff

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
with your dad and
how that woke you up

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.174
and then kind of changed the
course correction for you,

00:06:48.174 --> 00:06:50.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I think that's
a super interesting

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.244
part of the story here.

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Sure, so I write
about this in the book

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:56.149
as condensed as I can,
but basically my father

00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:57.784
was a major Democrat donor,

00:06:57.784 --> 00:07:00.486 align:start size:81% position:19%
New Jersey's a big
Democrat state.

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:03.156
He was involved in
a family dispute.

00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:04.824
It was a business
that he'd built up,

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.526
he'd brought his siblings in,

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:09.829 align:start size:97% position:3%
and basically he was
prosecuted for three years.

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:11.330
They couldn't really find much.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then he did something
maybe a little over-reacting

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:18.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of to get maybe
revenge on them

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:19.539
for causing so much pain to him,

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:22.208
which, you know, human
beings make mistakes.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:26.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he basically sent a
tape of his brother-in-law

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:29.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was always having
affairs with a prostitute

00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:31.083
and then sent it to his sister

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:32.685
just basically to say screw you.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.321
And then at the time, his
lawyers knew about it.

00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:37.089 align:start size:84% position:16%
They didn't think
that it was a crime,

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.491
but he didn't know
that his siblings

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:40.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
were then cooperating
witnesses with the government.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:41.994
So they arrested him.

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:44.297
Chris Christie was the
prosecutor at the time.

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:47.900
He made a big deal of
it, very ambitious.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.236
He ultimately went
on to be governor.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
And after that
happened, my father,

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:54.273
I write about the experience.

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:56.976
It was very reflective about it.

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:59.312
He said, look, I sinned
before God, before man.

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:00.246
I made a mistake.

00:08:00.246 --> 00:08:02.248
I let my power go to my head,

00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:05.017
and I shouldn't have done that.

00:08:05.017 --> 00:08:07.653
So he took a plea deal and
went to prison for a year.

00:08:08.688 --> 00:08:09.922
And it was very hard.

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:10.957
His company was a big
company at the time,

00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:12.859
had about 1,000 employees.

00:08:12.859 --> 00:08:15.928
And when he left,
I had to deal with,

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:18.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
I went to his office
five days a week.

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:22.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had great professionals
who stepped up

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:24.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
to run the company, but
you have a million people,

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:25.538 align:start size:88% position:13%
the government was
still sending subpoenas,

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.073
trying to target people.

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:30.142
It was very chaotic, and I'd
go and visit him every week,

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:32.278
or maybe I'd take a week
off every now and then,

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:34.447
but try to visit him
every week in prison

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:35.748
just to spend the time.

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:37.250
My father and I were very close,

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:41.220
we ran the marathon together,
and he's an amazing person.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.457
So I think you learn a
lot from that experience.

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:46.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
One thing he said
to me was, you know,

00:08:46.926 --> 00:08:49.128 align:start size:84% position:16%
life, sometimes
you're at 10,000 feet.

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:50.296
You have to come down to earth

00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:52.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
to really understand
what's there.

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:56.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I saw very high in
life, I saw very low in life.

00:08:56.736 --> 00:08:58.004
And going through
that experience,

00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:00.773
you realize that things
can get taken from you.

00:09:00.773 --> 00:09:02.742
Your freedom can
be taken from you,

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:04.644 align:start size:81% position:19%
your wealth can
be taken from you,

00:09:04.644 --> 00:09:07.046
your clothes can
be taken from you.

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.916
And what really matters
is just your friends,

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:11.484
your health, and what you do

00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:12.818
and what you make of
the time you have.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:16.589 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so it was a
fundamentally transformative

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:17.823
period of my life.

00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:19.725
And I write about
my reactions after,

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:22.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
and two specific things
that I write about,

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:25.498
number one is after
a couple of days

00:09:25.498 --> 00:09:29.068
of spending time with him
and getting that situation

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:31.270
as settled as it was gonna
get, I go back to New York

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:33.706 align:start size:97% position:3%
and got back to my
apartment on Mercer Street,

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:36.676 align:start position:0%
and I basically just sat on
the floor and I started crying.

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:40.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
I realized that my life
had changed, and I realized

00:09:40.046 --> 00:09:41.814
that there was a lot of
things I was angry at.

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:43.082
You're angry at the prosecutor,

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:45.151
you're angry at my
father's siblings

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:48.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
for escalating
something over money,

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:50.990
and then I was angry at
my father to a degree.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:53.225
I realized that that
just wasn't productive.

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:55.494 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then I really made
a decision to shift

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:58.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
all of my time and effort
towards trying to be helpful,

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:00.600
helpful to my mom,
helpful to my dad,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.102
helpful to my siblings,
helpful to the company.

00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:04.604
And that was just a
fundamental choice.

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:06.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that that's
been something in my life,

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.975
whether it was of the
challenges in business

00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:11.277
that occurred, or even
my time in government.

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:15.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
Any time there was a
challenge, a lot of people

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:17.516
get very angry or try to
figure out why it happened

00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:19.051
or what they could
have done different.

00:10:19.051 --> 00:10:21.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
I always would just say,
okay, well, it is what is is.

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:23.122
How do we take the
variables we have

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:25.191
and try to make it
as good as it can be?

00:10:25.191 --> 00:10:28.194
And so that was really
a fundamental lesson.

00:10:28.194 --> 00:10:30.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think the other
lesson I learned as well

00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:34.533
is I finally did my first day
back at the office at the DA.

00:10:36.836 --> 00:10:38.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would take the six
train down to Canal Street

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:43.009
from Astor Place, and
I was going back up,

00:10:43.009 --> 00:10:44.910
and for whatever reason
I just couldn't stand up,

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.346 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I just stayed on the
train, and I rode the train

00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:48.748
all the way up to
the Bronx and back

00:10:48.748 --> 00:10:50.683
for about usually
two to three hours,

00:10:50.683 --> 00:10:53.486
and I was just looking at
everyone's faces, right?

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:56.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're seeing the people
getting on and off the train.

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:58.491
For whatever reason, in
my mind I was saying,

00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:00.226
what's their story,
and I was kind of

00:11:00.226 --> 00:11:01.160
making up stories for each one.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.895
I was saying, well, this woman,

00:11:02.895 --> 00:11:06.666
maybe she can't find a
way to feed her family,

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:08.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
or maybe this guy's
about to lose his job,

00:11:08.467 --> 00:11:10.436 align:start size:84% position:16%
or maybe this woman
just found out

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:11.904
that her mother has cancer.

00:11:11.904 --> 00:11:15.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I kind of realized
everyone has problems in life,

00:11:15.141 --> 00:11:18.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I had my problems,
but everyone has problems.

00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:20.079
And so I kind of said, you know,

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:22.114
nobody wants to hear
about your problems.

00:11:23.315 --> 00:11:24.917
You've got to just do the best

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:26.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
with what you've got every
day and move forward.

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:29.188 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it was a very
interesting experience.

00:11:29.188 --> 00:11:32.725
I met a lot of people in
prison, or within prison.

00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:34.226 align:start size:84% position:16%
My father actually,
amazing people,

00:11:34.226 --> 00:11:37.329
still friends to this day,
from different backgrounds,

00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:39.965 align:start size:88% position:13%
some were bad people,
some were amazing people

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:41.734
who just made mistakes.

00:11:41.734 --> 00:11:45.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so it was a very
interesting period of life,

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:48.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one that obviously
taught me a lot.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
- What was it about
the prosecution

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:52.878
that caused you to have that
change and not go that route?

00:11:52.878 --> 00:11:54.080
Did you just see corruption?

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
Did you see an
endless goose chase?

00:11:56.749 --> 00:12:00.052
- So I saw, my father,
he was really the leader

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:01.554
of our community.

00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:04.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
Anyone who would lose a job,
he would give them a job.

00:12:04.457 --> 00:12:07.193 align:start position:0%
Anytime somebody needed a
surgery and couldn't afford it,

00:12:07.193 --> 00:12:09.228
he would pay for it anonymously.

00:12:09.228 --> 00:12:13.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
He supported so many people
and did so much good.

00:12:13.299 --> 00:12:15.868
When he was sentenced,
he had over 700 letters

00:12:15.868 --> 00:12:17.036
that were sent on his behalf.

00:12:17.036 --> 00:12:18.637 align:start size:88% position:13%
Each one was more
beautiful than the next,

00:12:18.637 --> 00:12:21.841
about stories that even his
family didn't know about.

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:25.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what I saw was that
they started investigating,

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:28.514 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then they were
looking for one thing.

00:12:28.514 --> 00:12:29.815
And then it just never ends.

00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:31.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then they keep digging
for more and more,

00:12:31.617 --> 00:12:33.652
and there was an accountant
who stole all the documents

00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:35.421
who now works for
my father's brother

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:37.890
who turned it over
to the government.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.859
And it just played
out in the press.

00:12:41.293 --> 00:12:42.628
It was funny, at the time one
of this friends said to me,

00:12:42.628 --> 00:12:44.630
there's an Irish saying
which is tall buildings

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:47.800
attract lightning, and
he was a tall building

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:49.268
and he attracted lightning.

00:12:49.268 --> 00:12:51.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
I saw just that prosecutors
have unlimited power,

00:12:51.837 --> 00:12:54.974
unlimited resources, and when
they want to dig, they'll dig.

00:12:54.974 --> 00:12:57.076
I mean, it was a
company that had

00:12:57.076 --> 00:12:58.911 align:start position:0%
hundreds of millions of
dollars of revenue at the time,

00:12:58.911 --> 00:13:00.479
and ultimately they
were saying, okay,

00:13:00.479 --> 00:13:02.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
you should have categorized
something this way

00:13:02.648 --> 00:13:05.217
instead of this way for a
couple of hundred thousand,

00:13:05.217 --> 00:13:06.852
and that was what
he kind of pled to.

00:13:06.852 --> 00:13:08.254
And that's pretty subjective,

00:13:08.254 --> 00:13:10.790
and usually something
that's done civilly

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.258
as opposed to criminally.

00:13:12.258 --> 00:13:15.027
So I just saw that Christie
wanted to get my father,

00:13:15.027 --> 00:13:17.863
he was a big trophy
politically for him,

00:13:17.863 --> 00:13:20.866 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he was just
absolutely relentless.

00:13:20.866 --> 00:13:25.204
But again, my father,
under so much pressure,

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:26.872
allowed his emotions to
get the better of him

00:13:26.872 --> 00:13:29.141
and made a mistake as well,
which was a lesson for me

00:13:29.141 --> 00:13:30.976
going through all
the investigations

00:13:30.976 --> 00:13:33.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we ultimately went
through as well in Washington,

00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:36.382
which was if you
have good facts,

00:13:36.382 --> 00:13:39.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
you just have to be
tight, be tight, be tight.

00:13:39.185 --> 00:13:41.020
And again, people saw
I didn't talk much.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:43.055
They saw me accused of
all kinds of things,

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:46.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I tried to just be
tight, be tight, be tight,

00:13:46.826 --> 00:13:51.163 align:start position:0%
just to know that I didn't think
my enemies could defeat me,

00:13:51.163 --> 00:13:53.265 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I knew that I was
capable of defeating myself

00:13:53.265 --> 00:13:56.936
by making a mistake, and I
didn't want that to happen.

00:13:56.936 --> 00:13:58.437
- Yeah, it's funny
you turned it to that

00:13:58.437 --> 00:13:59.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
because as you were saying
that, I was thinking,

00:13:59.672 --> 00:14:01.774
boy, this sort of endless chase

00:14:01.774 --> 00:14:05.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
and this power that they
have to keep going and going,

00:14:05.044 --> 00:14:06.312
that does sound awfully familiar

00:14:06.312 --> 00:14:08.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
to some things
happening these days.

00:14:08.714 --> 00:14:10.783
So obviously it's not
what the book's about,

00:14:10.783 --> 00:14:12.651 align:start size:97% position:3%
but just to knock out a
little bit of current events

00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:14.220
kind of quickly,
when you're seeing

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.722
what's happening right now,
the raid and all this stuff,

00:14:16.722 --> 00:14:18.390
does it ring very
familiar to you?

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.126
Like oh, we just
kind of have power

00:14:21.126 --> 00:14:22.928
and we can exercise that power,

00:14:22.928 --> 00:14:25.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then we'll figure
out if he did something.

00:14:25.297 --> 00:14:26.131
A little bit like that?

00:14:26.131 --> 00:14:27.166
- A thousand percent.

00:14:27.166 --> 00:14:28.434
You saw this with Trump.

00:14:28.434 --> 00:14:29.702
And again, I write
about in the book

00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:32.304
what it's like to be in
the middle of that storm

00:14:32.304 --> 00:14:35.541
and being accused of treason
and having to spend two years.

00:14:35.541 --> 00:14:38.177
At some point you're saying,
maybe I'm the one who's crazy,

00:14:38.177 --> 00:14:40.779
because I know I didn't
collude with Russia.

00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:42.348 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, most days I
couldn't collude with Trump

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:45.317
on the campaign, or the
handful of people we had.

00:14:45.317 --> 00:14:47.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a ragtag campaign,
and I write about

00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:49.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
how we pulled it
together and did that.

00:14:49.521 --> 00:14:51.590
But then you go to Washington.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
You have media sources
that you thought

00:14:54.193 --> 00:14:56.629
were these pristine,
amazing institutions

00:14:56.629 --> 00:14:58.264
like the New York Times
or the Washington Post

00:14:58.264 --> 00:15:00.132
or CNN or MSNBC.

00:15:00.132 --> 00:15:02.134
But every single day
and every single night,

00:15:02.134 --> 00:15:06.572 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're running more articles
or running more stories

00:15:06.572 --> 00:15:10.175 align:start size:88% position:13%
saying that the breaking
gun, breaking news.

00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:11.110
- [Dave] The walls
are crashing in.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:12.645
- The walls are crashing in.

00:15:12.645 --> 00:15:14.179
And I'd see it every day.

00:15:14.179 --> 00:15:17.716
I'd turn on Rachel Maddow
at night, and I was like,

00:15:17.716 --> 00:15:19.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
holy cow, do people
actually believe this?

00:15:19.551 --> 00:15:21.553
And I write actually
Schumer used to be

00:15:21.553 --> 00:15:23.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
a close friend of our family,
and he was going around

00:15:23.956 --> 00:15:26.325
the Upper East Side telling
all my parents' friends,

00:15:26.325 --> 00:15:28.160 align:start size:97% position:3%
Jared's about to be indicted,
he's gonna go to jail,

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:29.995
we know it for sure,
we have all the facts,

00:15:29.995 --> 00:15:31.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
what they did to
collude with Russia.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:33.332
They were basically
denying an election.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.670
But anyway, that
was definitely it.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.472
And I see it with Trump now,
where they tried one thing,

00:15:40.472 --> 00:15:42.508
it failed, they tried the
next thing, it failed.

00:15:42.508 --> 00:15:44.410
And then he leaves office.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.446
And then they impeach him
after he leaves office.

00:15:47.446 --> 00:15:51.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then now he's being looked
at by the Attorney General,

00:15:51.517 --> 00:15:54.520
even though there was
no money lost at all,

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:56.255
but they're saying maybe
his financial statements

00:15:56.255 --> 00:15:58.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
were either high or
low or whatever it is.

00:15:58.123 --> 00:16:00.893
Then they run these
primetime hearings

00:16:00.893 --> 00:16:02.895
basically to say what
a bad person he was.

00:16:02.895 --> 00:16:06.265
And then they raid his home.

00:16:06.265 --> 00:16:10.002
And so I do have
a lot of feelings

00:16:10.002 --> 00:16:12.805 align:start position:0%
that prosecutors left unchecked
with political ambitions,

00:16:12.805 --> 00:16:14.306 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that's why one of
the issues I write about

00:16:14.306 --> 00:16:17.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the book too is how,
because of my experiences,

00:16:17.076 --> 00:16:19.478
I spearheaded an effort
that President Trump

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:21.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
ultimately endorsed
and got behind

00:16:21.814 --> 00:16:23.282
to bring criminal
justice reform.

00:16:23.282 --> 00:16:26.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I do think that
his being so persecuted

00:16:26.452 --> 00:16:30.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
while he was in Washington
opened his heart to appreciate

00:16:30.089 --> 00:16:32.424
how unfair prosecutors can be.

00:16:32.424 --> 00:16:35.127
What bothers me a lot is
that all of the people

00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:37.262
who are my allies from the left

00:16:37.262 --> 00:16:39.064
against big government overreach

00:16:39.064 --> 00:16:41.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how unfair
prosecutors can be,

00:16:42.634 --> 00:16:44.136 align:start size:97% position:3%
they were amazing
advocates, they believe it,

00:16:44.136 --> 00:16:46.038
but when it happens
to Trump, it's okay.

00:16:46.038 --> 00:16:49.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so the moral lines
are a little bit,

00:16:49.375 --> 00:16:51.744
they have, I guess,
footnotes to it.

00:16:51.744 --> 00:16:54.113 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think that's pretty
much the definition

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:55.347
of Trump Derangement Syndrome,

00:16:55.347 --> 00:16:56.982
these people that have
this set of beliefs,

00:16:56.982 --> 00:16:58.217
but when it comes to Trump,

00:16:58.217 --> 00:17:00.352
those beliefs kind
of just don't exist.

00:17:00.352 --> 00:17:02.421
So going forward a little bit,

00:17:02.421 --> 00:17:03.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
before you got
fully into politics,

00:17:03.822 --> 00:17:05.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
so now you and
Ivanka are married,

00:17:05.958 --> 00:17:08.193
Donald signed off
on it obviously.

00:17:08.193 --> 00:17:09.928
And you're living a
life in New York City

00:17:09.928 --> 00:17:12.998
that's not political,
particularly, right?

00:17:12.998 --> 00:17:14.166
And then the call comes in.

00:17:14.166 --> 00:17:17.102
Okay, he's gonna do this thing.

00:17:17.102 --> 00:17:19.538 align:start size:97% position:3%
Did you immediately think
that you could be part of it

00:17:19.538 --> 00:17:22.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
or had value to add or that
Ivanka wanted to be part of it

00:17:22.674 --> 00:17:24.710
or were you worried, et cetera?

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:27.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So we didn't know, I
know this may sound weird,

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I would imagine most
families sit around and say,

00:17:30.282 --> 00:17:31.083
what should we do?

00:17:31.083 --> 00:17:32.317
This is a big decision.

00:17:32.317 --> 00:17:35.687 align:start size:88% position:13%
I write about we were
having lunch with Donald

00:17:35.687 --> 00:17:39.024 align:start position:0%
for I think it was his birthday,
and he says to the kids,

00:17:39.024 --> 00:17:41.693
he says, okay guys, I'm
announcing this week

00:17:41.693 --> 00:17:42.895
and everything's
gonna be different.

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:45.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
And with him, there's
always something,

00:17:45.531 --> 00:17:47.232
and he'd been flirting
with it for a long time.

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:50.069
He'd asked Ivanka to
write a speech for him.

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:51.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
And actually one
thing that was funny,

00:17:51.303 --> 00:17:53.439
she took him to go
see Rupert Murdoch,

00:17:53.439 --> 00:17:54.506 align:start size:88% position:13%
because she said, look,
if you're running,

00:17:54.506 --> 00:17:56.208
you've got to go sit with him.

00:17:56.208 --> 00:17:59.745 align:start position:0%
And Rupert says, you're not
running, you're not gonna do it.

00:17:59.745 --> 00:18:03.115 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Donald left and he says,
why was he so dismissive?

00:18:03.115 --> 00:18:05.951
And Ivanka says, look Dad,
you asked me to introduce you

00:18:05.951 --> 00:18:07.453
when you give your speech.

00:18:07.453 --> 00:18:09.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm still not sure
you're gonna announce.

00:18:09.254 --> 00:18:10.989
And she says, look, by the way,

00:18:10.989 --> 00:18:12.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you make me write this
speech and get ready,

00:18:12.758 --> 00:18:14.660
and I give this speech and
you get up there and say,

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:17.162
just kidding, I'll
be pretty mad at you.

00:18:17.162 --> 00:18:19.631
And so he kind of went into it.

00:18:19.631 --> 00:18:21.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then we also,
let's see what happens.

00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:24.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the first month
was just crazy.

00:18:24.303 --> 00:18:26.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, somebody tweeted
which I saw, which was funny,

00:18:26.972 --> 00:18:29.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
where they said the last
slow news day we had

00:18:29.541 --> 00:18:33.512
was June 15th, 2015, the
day before he announced.

00:18:33.512 --> 00:18:35.414
- The day before the escalator.

00:18:35.414 --> 00:18:37.950
- And he got up there.

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:39.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
The month after for
Ivanka was really consumed

00:18:39.918 --> 00:18:41.253
with putting out all the fires.

00:18:41.253 --> 00:18:43.255
I think Trump lost
about $30 million

00:18:43.255 --> 00:18:46.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
in just annual contracts
that were coming in

00:18:46.058 --> 00:18:48.927
with different vendors,
and her and her brothers

00:18:48.927 --> 00:18:50.262
were dealing with that.

00:18:50.262 --> 00:18:51.797
And I didn't really get involved

00:18:51.797 --> 00:18:54.366
until I guess it was later on.

00:18:54.366 --> 00:18:56.168
Donald said, come
with me to a rally.

00:18:56.168 --> 00:18:57.002
And I said, do you know what?

00:18:57.002 --> 00:18:58.203
Let me see this.

00:18:58.203 --> 00:19:00.572
Everyone in my social
circle in New York

00:19:00.572 --> 00:19:02.374
was saying this is a
joke, he's gonna lose.

00:19:02.374 --> 00:19:03.842
But every time that happened,

00:19:03.842 --> 00:19:06.578
he kept going up more
and more in the polls.

00:19:06.578 --> 00:19:08.914
And so I thought we were
in a very worldly circle,

00:19:08.914 --> 00:19:10.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're with the
heads of the banks,

00:19:10.149 --> 00:19:11.617
the heads of the
fashion industry,

00:19:11.617 --> 00:19:14.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
the heads of the technology,
the heads of the media.

00:19:14.186 --> 00:19:16.755
And I said, let me just go
see things with my own eyes.

00:19:16.755 --> 00:19:19.458
So I traveled with him
to Springfield, Illinois.

00:19:19.458 --> 00:19:23.028 align:start size:88% position:13%
We fly out in his big
757 Trump Force One jet.

00:19:24.429 --> 00:19:26.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're going out there,
and he's just bullshitting

00:19:26.632 --> 00:19:30.002
like he does, talking about
the grandkids and football.

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:32.604 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we walk out, and I have
no idea what to expect.

00:19:32.604 --> 00:19:34.540
We get into a car, go
to a convention center.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
We walk in, the gentleman
greets him and says,

00:19:36.775 --> 00:19:38.377
welcome Mr. Trump.

00:19:38.377 --> 00:19:39.878
Congratulations.

00:19:39.878 --> 00:19:41.947
You just broke the record
for this convention center.

00:19:41.947 --> 00:19:43.448
And he says, well,
who's the record?

00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:45.651
And he says, well, it was
Elton John 36 years ago.

00:19:45.651 --> 00:19:46.852
And he turns to me and he says,

00:19:46.852 --> 00:19:47.819
see, and I don't
even have a guitar.

00:19:47.819 --> 00:19:49.488
So we went in there.

00:19:49.488 --> 00:19:50.989
I also was surprised too.

00:19:50.989 --> 00:19:53.158
I saw him shaking everyone's
hand, and he was a germophobe

00:19:53.158 --> 00:19:55.794
before it was popular
to be a germophobe.

00:19:55.794 --> 00:19:57.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so he was shaking
everyone's hand,

00:19:57.129 --> 00:19:58.897 align:start size:84% position:16%
really engaging with
everyone he saw,

00:19:58.897 --> 00:20:01.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
trying to get
information from them.

00:20:01.266 --> 00:20:03.202 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he gets up on
stage for about an hour

00:20:03.202 --> 00:20:05.637
and just talks with
no notes or anything.

00:20:05.637 --> 00:20:08.440 align:start size:81% position:19%
And there's one
particular moment,

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:10.475
he was talking about trade,
where he was saying things

00:20:10.475 --> 00:20:11.643
that were contrary
to what my friends

00:20:11.643 --> 00:20:13.212
in the Upper East Side thought.

00:20:13.212 --> 00:20:14.680 align:start size:81% position:19%
But he gets up
there and he says,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna end common core
and we're gonna send it

00:20:16.114 --> 00:20:17.883
to the states, and
the crowd went wild.

00:20:17.883 --> 00:20:19.785
And keep in mind,
the crowd was not

00:20:19.785 --> 00:20:21.620 align:start size:84% position:16%
what CNN told you
what it was gonna be.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:25.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
They made it seem like it
was Ku Klux Klan or whatever.

00:20:25.023 --> 00:20:29.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was old, young, white,
Black, male, female,

00:20:30.495 --> 00:20:33.732
and it just felt like
it was real Americans

00:20:33.732 --> 00:20:36.401
who felt like the government
had stopped representing them.

00:20:36.401 --> 00:20:39.538
And so a few weeks earlier
I had been at a charity gala

00:20:39.538 --> 00:20:41.240 align:start size:84% position:16%
in New York for the
Robin Hood Foundation,

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.110 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is one of these great
groups that does amazing work.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:47.646
And the president of it
gets up there and says

00:20:47.646 --> 00:20:50.082
the way we are gonna create
equality in this country

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:54.152
and save our country is
by promoting common core,

00:20:54.152 --> 00:20:56.088
and what everyone has to
do is call their senators,

00:20:56.088 --> 00:20:57.089
call their Congressman.

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:57.956
This is the policy.

00:20:57.956 --> 00:20:58.790
This is it.

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:00.259
This is gonna change.

00:21:00.259 --> 00:21:01.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like, okay, I guess
common core is good.

00:21:01.193 --> 00:21:02.361
I mean, what did I know?

00:21:02.361 --> 00:21:03.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was doing real
estate and technology.

00:21:03.895 --> 00:21:05.998
I wasn't deep in the policies.

00:21:05.998 --> 00:21:07.766
So it kind of made me think,

00:21:07.766 --> 00:21:09.601
wait, I thought all
these worldly people

00:21:09.601 --> 00:21:10.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
knew what they
were talking about.

00:21:10.869 --> 00:21:12.838
Why do these people feel
so differently to them?

00:21:12.838 --> 00:21:16.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so all it did was
really start my exploration

00:21:16.541 --> 00:21:17.976
through the process.

00:21:17.976 --> 00:21:20.812 align:start position:0%
- What did you consider yourself
politically at that point,

00:21:20.812 --> 00:21:23.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you were sort of
having your own wake up

00:21:23.515 --> 00:21:25.017
relative to some of this stuff?

00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:27.586
Did you consider yourself
like a New York liberal,

00:21:27.586 --> 00:21:31.490
like I was an Upper West Side
liberal, I was for many years.

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:33.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm obviously not
anymore, but did you?

00:21:33.258 --> 00:21:34.760
- I wouldn't say that.

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:35.861
When I was Harvard, I was
probably much more liberal.

00:21:35.861 --> 00:21:37.062
That's what you're taught.

00:21:37.062 --> 00:21:39.197
You take Ec 10 with
Marty Feldstein,

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:45.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you have certain views
of how the world works.

00:21:46.238 --> 00:21:48.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
I started off doing
kind of social studies,

00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:50.942
so it was reading a lot of
the political theorists.

00:21:52.778 --> 00:21:56.114
But through the newspaper,
we endorsed Obama in 2008.

00:21:56.114 --> 00:21:58.317
And then in 2012,
we endorsed Romney,

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:01.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I thought that Obama's
policies were very divisive

00:22:01.186 --> 00:22:04.423
and I thought that
he was terrible with
his Middle East work

00:22:04.423 --> 00:22:07.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
especially how he was going
to try to do a deal with Iran,

00:22:07.693 --> 00:22:10.028
and I felt like he
was really weakening

00:22:10.028 --> 00:22:12.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
the U.S. Israel relationship,
which I didn't like.

00:22:14.099 --> 00:22:15.600
And so I endorsed him.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.102
And I was very close with
Mike Bloomberg at the time.

00:22:17.102 --> 00:22:19.938 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought he represented,
he was probably

00:22:19.938 --> 00:22:23.041
the best embodiment of
my political package.

00:22:23.041 --> 00:22:25.377
So I was registered
as an independent,

00:22:25.377 --> 00:22:27.612
but probably at that
point very centrist.

00:22:27.612 --> 00:22:32.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
But like I said, it
was kind of this deep.

00:22:32.117 --> 00:22:34.820 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I learned too, most
people are actually this deep.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:36.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
One of the cool
things about the book

00:22:36.054 --> 00:22:38.223
is I take people
through my journey

00:22:38.223 --> 00:22:40.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
as somebody who was
from that echo chamber

00:22:40.592 --> 00:22:43.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then went and
explored the country.

00:22:43.095 --> 00:22:46.231
Most people don't get to meet
people who disagree with them

00:22:46.231 --> 00:22:47.733
and don't engage with them.

00:22:47.733 --> 00:22:49.968
And I got to speak
with so many people,

00:22:49.968 --> 00:22:51.503
but I did it with an open mind

00:22:51.503 --> 00:22:53.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I was having to
write speeches at the time

00:22:53.038 --> 00:22:56.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
for Donald on the campaign
that were representing

00:22:56.241 --> 00:22:57.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
his points of view,
which were points of view

00:22:57.743 --> 00:22:59.344
that were different
than what I thought

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:01.413
was the right point
of view to have.

00:23:01.413 --> 00:23:03.115
So in doing that, I was
exploring these things,

00:23:03.115 --> 00:23:06.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
researching these things,
and it opened my eyes

00:23:06.351 --> 00:23:08.453
to the fact there's
a lot more nuance

00:23:08.453 --> 00:23:09.755
to a lot of these policies.

00:23:09.755 --> 00:23:11.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So then the
campaign's starting.

00:23:11.923 --> 00:23:13.458
I've talked to Don
Junior about this

00:23:13.458 --> 00:23:15.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
publicly on this
show, and privately.

00:23:15.594 --> 00:23:17.262
He was telling me
how so many people

00:23:17.262 --> 00:23:18.797 align:start size:81% position:19%
that were lifelong
friends of his

00:23:18.797 --> 00:23:21.466
suddenly deserted him and
just all of that nonsense.

00:23:21.466 --> 00:23:24.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
So now you and
Ivanka are intimately
involved in this thing,

00:23:24.336 --> 00:23:25.270
and then he wins.

00:23:25.270 --> 00:23:26.605
Now you're going to DC.

00:23:26.605 --> 00:23:28.106
I mean, first off,
did you have just

00:23:28.106 --> 00:23:30.342
a whole bunch of friends
that shed you immediately,

00:23:30.342 --> 00:23:32.711
and how insane was
just getting to DC

00:23:32.711 --> 00:23:34.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then seeing
what that was like?

00:23:34.646 --> 00:23:36.314 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So believe it
or not, we didn't,

00:23:36.314 --> 00:23:38.583
and I think that
what happened was

00:23:38.583 --> 00:23:40.919
is I think through my
father's experience,

00:23:40.919 --> 00:23:44.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was very selective
in who I let in.

00:23:44.222 --> 00:23:47.359
So Ivanka and I have a
very close relationship,

00:23:47.359 --> 00:23:49.227
and a lot of our time
in New York was spent

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:50.695
just the two of us.

00:23:50.695 --> 00:23:51.930 align:start size:97% position:3%
If we had to choose between
going to a dinner party

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
or being with us,
we would be with us,

00:23:53.532 --> 00:23:55.333
and we would always love
doing different adventures,

00:23:55.333 --> 00:23:57.369 align:start size:84% position:16%
going on dates, trying
new restaurants,

00:23:57.369 --> 00:23:59.070
seeing new neighborhoods.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:00.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then as we started
building a family,

00:24:00.806 --> 00:24:02.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was a priority
for us as well.

00:24:02.507 --> 00:24:05.076 align:start size:97% position:3%
We'd had a ton of
acquaintances from New York,

00:24:05.076 --> 00:24:07.579
but the people who
are our real friends

00:24:07.579 --> 00:24:09.948
were people who we thought
were very quality people

00:24:09.948 --> 00:24:12.818
who we got things
out of, and I think

00:24:12.818 --> 00:24:14.352
who got things out of us.

00:24:14.352 --> 00:24:17.088
And those people were all
people mostly on the left

00:24:17.088 --> 00:24:18.590
of the political spectrum.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:20.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
Again, I probably had like
one conservative friend

00:24:20.592 --> 00:24:24.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I could think of from
before this experience.

00:24:24.129 --> 00:24:26.531 align:start size:88% position:13%
But everyone was very
respectful and very open

00:24:26.531 --> 00:24:29.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
of what we were doing,
and recognized that again,

00:24:29.601 --> 00:24:31.970
it wasn't Ivanka and I
who were giving speeches

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:33.238
or making policies.

00:24:33.238 --> 00:24:34.873
It was her father,
and she was doing

00:24:34.873 --> 00:24:37.742 align:start size:81% position:19%
what a devoted
daughter would do.

00:24:38.877 --> 00:24:41.313 align:start size:84% position:16%
But for me, I was
much more open-minded,

00:24:41.313 --> 00:24:45.417
much more studying the policies.

00:24:45.417 --> 00:24:47.586
I think for Ivanka,
she basically said,

00:24:47.586 --> 00:24:50.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
okay, if this is the
situation that's happening,

00:24:50.422 --> 00:24:51.990 align:start size:88% position:13%
she had a mission-driven
business at the time,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:55.093
which there were issues
she was advocating for.

00:24:55.093 --> 00:24:57.429
She said, well, if I can
use this as a platform

00:24:57.429 --> 00:24:59.164 align:start size:84% position:16%
to elevate the issues
she cared about,

00:24:59.164 --> 00:25:03.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
like paid family
leave and child care,

00:25:03.201 --> 00:25:06.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
she says, well, I think
it's kind of irresponsible

00:25:06.371 --> 00:25:07.906
not to do that.

00:25:07.906 --> 00:25:10.208
So she gave a speech at the
RNC introducing her father,

00:25:10.208 --> 00:25:12.210
and all the things on the
internet after were saying

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:14.079
did she think she was at
the Democratic convention,

00:25:14.079 --> 00:25:17.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they were
not traditionally
Republican policies.

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.653
- And that her dad used to
be loved by the Democrats.

00:25:21.653 --> 00:25:23.855
I mean, there's no
shortage of pictures

00:25:23.855 --> 00:25:25.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all those people when
they used to love him,

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:27.659
and he was donating
to some of them.

00:25:27.659 --> 00:25:29.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, being in
New York, and again,

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:30.695
he was part of pop culture.

00:25:30.695 --> 00:25:33.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, but the short
answer is that our friends

00:25:33.598 --> 00:25:37.969
were really strong
and really still are.

00:25:37.969 --> 00:25:41.973
What I found is that when we
decided to go to Washington,

00:25:41.973 --> 00:25:43.842
and I write about kind of
how we made that decision,

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:45.343 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it wasn't
like we were thinking,

00:25:45.343 --> 00:25:46.711
okay, he's gonna win and
then this is gonna happen.

00:25:46.711 --> 00:25:48.313
The whole campaign was like,

00:25:48.313 --> 00:25:50.115
let's get to goal
line plus an inch.

00:25:50.115 --> 00:25:52.083
Who the hell knows how
this is gonna work out?

00:25:52.083 --> 00:25:53.351
- But you do talk about that.

00:25:53.351 --> 00:25:54.319
You kind of thought
it was gonna happen

00:25:54.319 --> 00:25:55.954
the whole time, right?

00:25:55.954 --> 00:25:59.124 align:start position:0%
- Again, logically it felt like
he should be able to do it.

00:25:59.124 --> 00:26:02.127 align:start size:97% position:3%
All the math, and I can go
into a whole thing on polling.

00:26:02.127 --> 00:26:04.996
But from what I saw,
the polling all seemed

00:26:04.996 --> 00:26:06.898
like it was not scientific.

00:26:06.898 --> 00:26:08.366
So if you look at all
the public polling

00:26:08.366 --> 00:26:09.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
that predicted he was
gonna lose, I was shocked

00:26:09.935 --> 00:26:11.703
by how many people
just followed it.

00:26:11.703 --> 00:26:15.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
But polling is basically
you call 500 people

00:26:15.006 --> 00:26:17.042
and they say yes or no, and
then you have to do something

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:19.778 align:start position:0%
called a weighting, and
weighting is basically you say,

00:26:19.778 --> 00:26:24.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
okay, if we called 100
Democrats and 100 Republicans,

00:26:24.616 --> 00:26:25.917
how many people do you
think are gonna vote,

00:26:25.917 --> 00:26:29.020
and then you kind of
recalculate the results

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:30.488
in order to do that.

00:26:30.488 --> 00:26:31.923
So what happened in Brexit,
right, before Trump won,

00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:34.859 align:start size:81% position:19%
was the polling
was off massively.

00:26:34.859 --> 00:26:36.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, it predicted
the amount of people

00:26:36.895 --> 00:26:38.763
who said stay pretty accurately,

00:26:38.763 --> 00:26:40.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it was off by about
three million votes

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:43.001
for the people who
predicted to leave.

00:26:43.001 --> 00:26:45.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as we researched
why that was,

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:47.672
I was reading all
the info on it,

00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:49.841
it basically said that
they just under counted

00:26:49.841 --> 00:26:51.076
low propensity voters.

00:26:51.076 --> 00:26:54.212
So with Trump, all
the media was using

00:26:54.212 --> 00:26:56.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically the Obama/Romney
turnout model,

00:26:56.615 --> 00:26:58.583
and I was like, I don't
think that'd indicative.

00:26:58.583 --> 00:27:01.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
Obama had a different
electricity that
Hillary didn't have,

00:27:01.186 --> 00:27:03.555 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Trump had a
different electricity

00:27:03.555 --> 00:27:04.789
that Romney didn't have.

00:27:04.789 --> 00:27:06.524
So it felt like it
should be different,

00:27:06.524 --> 00:27:09.060
and then when I'd look
at, we built a data file

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.495 align:start size:94% position:6%
of about 10 million
people who were showing up

00:27:10.495 --> 00:27:13.031
at Trump rallies, and a
big percentage of them

00:27:13.031 --> 00:27:14.299
were low propensity voters.

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:16.901
And so basically every
time we'd do a sample,

00:27:16.901 --> 00:27:19.270 align:start size:84% position:16%
which we re-engineered
the polling,

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:20.872 align:start size:97% position:3%
we basically ran it through
two different models.

00:27:20.872 --> 00:27:23.241 align:start size:97% position:3%
One was the media model, which
showed we were gonna lose,

00:27:23.241 --> 00:27:24.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
and one was our model, which
showed we were gonna win.

00:27:24.843 --> 00:27:28.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I just, again, Romney
went into election night

00:27:28.313 --> 00:27:30.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
thinking they were
gonna win, I was told,

00:27:30.382 --> 00:27:32.017
and then were disappointed.

00:27:32.017 --> 00:27:34.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I never allowed myself
to believe our data

00:27:34.185 --> 00:27:35.654
because I'd never
done it before.

00:27:35.654 --> 00:27:39.924
But that was what I believed
was more logical as an outcome,

00:27:39.924 --> 00:27:42.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we didn't think about
what would come next.

00:27:42.093 --> 00:27:43.294
Sorry for such a long thing.

00:27:43.294 --> 00:27:44.729
- No, no, so speaking
of what came next,

00:27:44.729 --> 00:27:47.999 align:start position:0%
so now you get there, now you're
working for the President.

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:51.002 align:start size:97% position:3%
The media's not that
happy, CNN, New York Times,

00:27:51.002 --> 00:27:52.637
okay, all that stuff.

00:27:52.637 --> 00:27:55.840
But you guys actually were
accomplishing an awful lot

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:57.575
pretty much from the get
go, considering you had

00:27:57.575 --> 00:28:00.779
nobody willing to work with you.

00:28:00.779 --> 00:28:02.947
- Yeah, so it was a wild thing.

00:28:02.947 --> 00:28:04.916
The first year, in
writing the book

00:28:04.916 --> 00:28:07.585
I went through the first
year, and it brought back

00:28:07.585 --> 00:28:11.156 align:start position:0%
a lot of memories that actually
were really painful for me,

00:28:11.156 --> 00:28:13.091
because A, we get to Washington.

00:28:13.091 --> 00:28:15.293
So we make the decision
to, and it wasn't like

00:28:15.293 --> 00:28:17.395
should we stay in New
York and live our lives

00:28:17.395 --> 00:28:18.663
or should we go to Washington?

00:28:18.663 --> 00:28:19.898
It was like, okay,
he is now gonna be

00:28:19.898 --> 00:28:21.366
the President of
the United States.

00:28:21.366 --> 00:28:24.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
Our former lives as we knew
it don't exist anymore.

00:28:24.536 --> 00:28:26.938
And then you see you have
an opportunity to serve

00:28:26.938 --> 00:28:29.808
in government and try
to make an impact,

00:28:29.808 --> 00:28:32.110 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I built more confidence
based on the fact

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:34.679
that on the campaign I was able
to achieve certain successes

00:28:34.679 --> 00:28:37.949
and things that experienced
political people told me

00:28:37.949 --> 00:28:39.984
was the wrong way to do it,
but then I did it that way

00:28:39.984 --> 00:28:42.053
and it ended up working.

00:28:42.053 --> 00:28:43.555
And so I said, well,
let's give it a shot

00:28:43.555 --> 00:28:45.323
and let's try to help him.

00:28:45.323 --> 00:28:48.059 align:start size:97% position:3%
Again, he'd never been a
mayor, never been a governor.

00:28:48.059 --> 00:28:49.894
He was running a
family business,

00:28:49.894 --> 00:28:51.396 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was used to
working with family,

00:28:51.396 --> 00:28:53.865
his campaign was
a family campaign.

00:28:53.865 --> 00:28:56.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we said let's
go give it a shot

00:28:56.101 --> 00:28:58.737
and really try to see if
we could help him implement

00:28:58.737 --> 00:29:00.739
the policies that we believe in

00:29:00.739 --> 00:29:02.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
and try to do things
that we believe are good.

00:29:02.507 --> 00:29:05.577
So Ivanka gave up her business
to then focus on her mission.

00:29:05.577 --> 00:29:10.181
I went in, and we had no
idea what a brutal world

00:29:10.181 --> 00:29:11.416
Washington was gonna be.

00:29:11.416 --> 00:29:14.953
So whether it was the
attacks from the media,

00:29:14.953 --> 00:29:16.387 align:start size:81% position:19%
whether it was
the investigation.

00:29:16.387 --> 00:29:19.624 align:start position:0%
Within three months I have
people leaking on me like crazy

00:29:19.624 --> 00:29:20.792
from inside the White House.

00:29:20.792 --> 00:29:22.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
You'd talk to yourself
and it would be

00:29:22.193 --> 00:29:24.162
in the Washington Post.

00:29:24.162 --> 00:29:25.497
- Wait, can you just
talk a little bit more

00:29:25.497 --> 00:29:28.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
about what that's like,
the leak situation?

00:29:28.366 --> 00:29:30.301
How is it possible, because
everyone's always talking

00:29:30.301 --> 00:29:31.970
about the leaks, they
were talking about it

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:33.571 align:start size:84% position:16%
an awful lot with
Trump, but it happens

00:29:33.571 --> 00:29:35.440
in every administration.

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:39.144 align:start size:97% position:3%
Did you feel like, oh, here's
10 people in this room,

00:29:39.144 --> 00:29:43.248
in this meeting, six of
them might be the one.

00:29:43.248 --> 00:29:45.817
How do you manage some of
that stuff as an adviser?

00:29:45.817 --> 00:29:48.153
Or do you pull him aside
and say, Mr. President,

00:29:48.153 --> 00:29:50.355
maybe don't, or Dad-in-law,
or whatever you call him,

00:29:50.355 --> 00:29:53.091
Don, do you maybe don't
say everything you think

00:29:53.091 --> 00:29:54.325 align:start size:81% position:19%
off the cuff in
front of everybody

00:29:54.325 --> 00:29:55.860
because we have this problem?

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:58.062
- So I think I learned, and
I think he learned as well.

00:29:58.062 --> 00:29:59.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, one of the funny
jokes somebody told me

00:29:59.898 --> 00:30:02.066
was they said in Washington,
if you want to keep a secret

00:30:02.066 --> 00:30:04.569
between three people, two
of them have to be dead.

00:30:06.004 --> 00:30:07.438
But it wasn't quite that bad.

00:30:07.438 --> 00:30:09.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it was a function
of in the beginning

00:30:09.107 --> 00:30:10.542
I felt very, very helpless.

00:30:10.542 --> 00:30:11.843
Like I said, I'd think something

00:30:11.843 --> 00:30:13.444
and it would leak to
the Washington Post.

00:30:13.444 --> 00:30:15.880
It was so bad, and I
think with him too.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:17.081
So a couple of things.

00:30:17.081 --> 00:30:19.450 align:start size:88% position:13%
Again, I write about
how I adjusted and said,

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.451
okay, the game is the game.

00:30:20.451 --> 00:30:22.120
It's been here long before me,

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:23.521 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's gonna be here
long after me.

00:30:23.521 --> 00:30:25.190
You have a chance
to be in the game.

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:27.959
If you are in the game, you
can get good things done,

00:30:27.959 --> 00:30:29.494
but you have to get
better at the game.

00:30:29.494 --> 00:30:32.564
And so certain things like
when I would give my opinion

00:30:32.564 --> 00:30:34.499
to Donald, especially when
I would disagree with him,

00:30:34.499 --> 00:30:36.034
I would always do it one on one.

00:30:36.034 --> 00:30:39.637 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would never say my opinion
in front of other people.

00:30:39.637 --> 00:30:41.773 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or I would say something
that I didn't think

00:30:41.773 --> 00:30:44.809
would be salacious, but when
I wanted to strongly disagree,

00:30:44.809 --> 00:30:46.377
I would always do it privately

00:30:46.377 --> 00:30:48.179
because you never wanted to
read about that in the press

00:30:48.179 --> 00:30:51.749
because that would, I
never wanted division

00:30:51.749 --> 00:30:55.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
to show between us
because he always gave me

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:57.188
the opportunity to
voice my opinion,

00:30:57.188 --> 00:30:59.224 align:start size:97% position:3%
and sometimes he would
listen, sometimes he wouldn't.

00:30:59.224 --> 00:31:01.092
Again, I was an adviser.

00:31:01.092 --> 00:31:03.094 align:start position:0%
And some things, quite frankly,
I had more conviction on,

00:31:03.094 --> 00:31:04.329
some things I had
less conviction on.

00:31:04.329 --> 00:31:05.897
And often when he would
ask me, I would say,

00:31:05.897 --> 00:31:07.799
look, I'm not an expert on
it, let me find somebody

00:31:07.799 --> 00:31:09.300
who I think is,
and bring them in

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:10.835
to give you that point of view.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:13.872
So that was probably number one.

00:31:13.872 --> 00:31:16.174
And then when it came to
just dealing with people,

00:31:16.174 --> 00:31:20.511
you learn very quickly who
was leaking to which places.

00:31:20.511 --> 00:31:22.814
We just had so many of
them who were scumbags

00:31:22.814 --> 00:31:24.249
who were doing that.

00:31:24.249 --> 00:31:26.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But were they all your
guys, do you know what I mean,

00:31:26.918 --> 00:31:29.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
or were they sort
of deep state people

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:30.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were all
there all the time?

00:31:30.121 --> 00:31:31.322
- No, no, no.

00:31:31.322 --> 00:31:32.790
A lot of them were
the people coming in.

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:35.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
Look, I mean we didn't have
a lot of people, right?

00:31:35.994 --> 00:31:38.196
The campaign was ragtag.

00:31:38.196 --> 00:31:40.131
We had to staff up quickly.

00:31:40.131 --> 00:31:41.466
We were making a
lot of decisions,

00:31:41.466 --> 00:31:43.268
putting different
people together,

00:31:43.268 --> 00:31:44.736
and yeah, you just have people.

00:31:44.736 --> 00:31:46.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then the more
people get insecure,

00:31:46.437 --> 00:31:48.273
the more it's an environment

00:31:48.273 --> 00:31:50.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
that kind of
encourages more of it.

00:31:50.008 --> 00:31:53.778 align:start position:0%
So it's a very, very complicated
situation to work in.

00:31:53.778 --> 00:31:57.181
But like I said, I figured out
how to adjust, I got tighter,

00:31:57.181 --> 00:31:59.450
and then ultimately over
time, like towards the end,

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:01.953 align:start size:97% position:3%
we announce a peace deal
which we'll talk about later,

00:32:01.953 --> 00:32:03.955
and nobody knows about it.

00:32:03.955 --> 00:32:06.858
So I learned how to find the
people who you could work with

00:32:06.858 --> 00:32:09.627
to keep things quiet, but
it just took me some time.

00:32:09.627 --> 00:32:12.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like when you work in
real estate or technology,

00:32:12.397 --> 00:32:13.998
nobody's out there
leaking to the press.

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:16.334
And everyone's aligned
for the same mission too.

00:32:16.334 --> 00:32:18.469
So it's just a different game

00:32:18.469 --> 00:32:20.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you just have to learn
to adjust to the game.

00:32:20.505 --> 00:32:23.808
- Right, so all right, let's
jump over to the peace process.

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:26.744
So now things are happening,
you're a couple of years in.

00:32:26.744 --> 00:32:31.749
You were working on this
from day one basically?

00:32:32.884 --> 00:32:34.285
Really, was it from
day one you said

00:32:34.285 --> 00:32:35.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is gonna be something
I'm gonna get on?

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:37.155
Did he want you on that?

00:32:37.155 --> 00:32:39.557
Was this something that
you just brought to him?

00:32:39.557 --> 00:32:44.562
- So the way I found out
about it was I get a call

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:47.665
from the woman who
did public relations

00:32:47.665 --> 00:32:50.101
for our real estate business.

00:32:50.101 --> 00:32:52.470
And at the time Ivanka
and I were still thinking

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:54.572
will we go to Washington or not?

00:32:54.572 --> 00:32:57.108
And she calls and says, the
New York Times just called

00:32:57.108 --> 00:32:59.010
and said you and Ivanka have
decided to go to Washington,

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:00.445
that you're working
on Middle East peace.

00:33:00.445 --> 00:33:01.612
And I said, that's not true.

00:33:01.612 --> 00:33:03.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
I said that we haven't
made a decision.

00:33:03.047 --> 00:33:04.449 align:start size:97% position:3%
She says, well, their source
is your father-in-law.

00:33:04.449 --> 00:33:05.984
I said, okay.

00:33:05.984 --> 00:33:08.920
So he went there for I guess
an editorial board meeting,

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:10.154
and they asked him.

00:33:10.154 --> 00:33:11.389
He said, ah, Jared's
great, he's gonna come

00:33:11.389 --> 00:33:12.190
and he's gonna work
on Middle East peace.

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:13.424
- Oh, that's hilarious.

00:33:13.424 --> 00:33:14.892
- I don't know if
he meant it or not,

00:33:14.892 --> 00:33:17.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the next day they're
going, who is Jared Kushner?

00:33:17.328 --> 00:33:18.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
What the hell does he know
about the Middle East?

00:33:18.896 --> 00:33:20.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
By the way, they were right,
I didn't know anything.

00:33:20.164 --> 00:33:22.166
Maybe it was the last
time they were right.

00:33:23.634 --> 00:33:25.403 align:start size:88% position:13%
But then after the
first couple of months,

00:33:25.403 --> 00:33:27.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
I said, okay, this is
something I was tasked with,

00:33:27.905 --> 00:33:29.440
let me start exploring.

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:33.378
So Jason Greenblatt came in
with me, and Avi Berkowitz.

00:33:33.378 --> 00:33:37.448
We started just researching
what had been done before us.

00:33:37.448 --> 00:33:40.451
What I did in the first
year was I just tried

00:33:40.451 --> 00:33:42.653
to understand everyone's
perspective on the file.

00:33:42.653 --> 00:33:45.456
So my biggest deficit
was that I didn't have

00:33:45.456 --> 00:33:47.725
any diplomatic experience,
but my biggest advantage

00:33:47.725 --> 00:33:49.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
was that I didn't have
any diplomatic experience.

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:52.697 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I came in, and I
give one example in here

00:33:52.697 --> 00:33:54.632
where I'm meeting with
the ruler of the UAE,

00:33:54.632 --> 00:33:57.769
who's thought to be the
most strategic thinker

00:33:57.769 --> 00:33:59.070
in the region.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:00.471
He's somebody who's
been there a while.

00:34:00.471 --> 00:34:02.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just became the
president of the country.

00:34:02.006 --> 00:34:05.243
And he's a guy, Mohamed bin
Zayed, who's really a giant.

00:34:05.243 --> 00:34:07.979
And I'm meeting with him
after about six months

00:34:07.979 --> 00:34:10.148
and I take him through
some of the thoughts

00:34:10.148 --> 00:34:12.283
as to how I want to approach it,

00:34:12.283 --> 00:34:14.385
and I really was asking
him a ton of questions.

00:34:14.385 --> 00:34:17.522 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he says, I think you're
gonna make peace here.

00:34:17.522 --> 00:34:19.123
Why do you say that?

00:34:19.123 --> 00:34:20.992
He says, well, the
U.S. usually sends

00:34:20.992 --> 00:34:23.061
one of three types
of people to see me.

00:34:23.061 --> 00:34:24.295 align:start size:84% position:16%
The first types
are people who come

00:34:24.295 --> 00:34:26.164
and fall asleep in meetings.

00:34:26.164 --> 00:34:27.732
The second are people who come

00:34:27.732 --> 00:34:29.567 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they basically
read me talking points

00:34:29.567 --> 00:34:32.570
and have no ability to
have a conversation.

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:34.539
The third are senior
level people who come

00:34:34.539 --> 00:34:36.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
and basically try to
convince me to do things

00:34:36.541 --> 00:34:38.209
that are against my interests.

00:34:38.209 --> 00:34:40.311 align:start position:0%
He says you're the first person
who's ever come to see me

00:34:40.311 --> 00:34:43.214
from the U.S. who's
asking questions.

00:34:43.214 --> 00:34:45.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my fundamental
question was very simple,

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:47.385
which is the U.S.
has a ton of power.

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:51.689
What would you do
if you were us?

00:34:51.689 --> 00:34:54.092
What's the end state
that's achievable here,

00:34:54.092 --> 00:34:56.894
and what levers would you
play with if you were us

00:34:56.894 --> 00:34:58.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
in order to try to
achieve that end state?

00:34:58.529 --> 00:35:00.364 align:start position:0%
And it took him a couple of
times of me asking that question

00:35:00.364 --> 00:35:02.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
to actually understand
that I was seeking

00:35:02.834 --> 00:35:05.236
honest feedback on the question.

00:35:05.236 --> 00:35:06.671 align:start size:84% position:16%
But that was how I
started with everyone,

00:35:06.671 --> 00:35:09.474
trying to understand,
get their point of view

00:35:09.474 --> 00:35:12.009
on what needed to be
done, knowing that

00:35:12.009 --> 00:35:13.611 align:start size:88% position:13%
American foreign
policy, and this is not

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:15.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
a knock on Democrats,
it's a knock on Democrats

00:35:15.413 --> 00:35:17.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Republicans,
basically screwed up

00:35:17.515 --> 00:35:18.950
the entire Middle East.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:22.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you take it from allowing,
even just from the fall

00:35:22.386 --> 00:35:26.691
of the shaw in Iran to
the revolution in '79,

00:35:26.691 --> 00:35:29.427 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then you go into
obviously allowing al Qaeda

00:35:29.427 --> 00:35:31.796
to have harbor, and then
the attacks on the U.S.

00:35:31.796 --> 00:35:33.831
and then getting us
into a war in Iraq

00:35:33.831 --> 00:35:35.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a war in Afghanistan,
and then you have

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:36.701
disbanding the army.

00:35:37.602 --> 00:35:39.070
It goes on and on.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:41.939
Then you have ISIS has a
caliphate the size of Ohio,

00:35:41.939 --> 00:35:45.276 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're beheading journalists,
they're killing Christians.

00:35:46.544 --> 00:35:49.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have them radicalizing
people online,

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:52.850
the San Bernardino shooting,
the Pulse Night Club shooting.

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:55.453
Syria's in a civil war, half
a million people are killed.

00:35:55.453 --> 00:35:58.456
I mean, U.S. policy in
that region for decades

00:35:58.456 --> 00:35:59.690
was absolutely awful.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.059
And part of it, I guess
maybe Trump was saying

00:36:02.059 --> 00:36:04.795
it can't get any worse,
so give Jared a try.

00:36:04.795 --> 00:36:07.431
But we thought about
it in a different way,

00:36:07.431 --> 00:36:08.833
and really worked hard.

00:36:08.833 --> 00:36:11.335 align:start position:0%
I write about kind of my
journey trying to understand it

00:36:11.335 --> 00:36:13.371
for what it was at the time,

00:36:13.371 --> 00:36:16.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
instead of being romanced
by the old notions.

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:18.910
And then the more I would talk
to the people who'd done it,

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:20.578
they would explain well, this
is what you have to look at,

00:36:20.578 --> 00:36:22.046
this is what you have to do.

00:36:22.046 --> 00:36:23.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like, that makes
absolutely no sense to me.

00:36:23.247 --> 00:36:24.815
And I was like, okay,
if I'm gonna fail,

00:36:24.815 --> 00:36:28.152 align:start position:0%
at least I'll fail in an
original way in doing something

00:36:28.152 --> 00:36:31.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I think is logical,
and I was creamed.

00:36:31.022 --> 00:36:33.291
I mean, the New York
Times would kill me,

00:36:33.291 --> 00:36:36.227
all the experts on CNN,
they would write articles

00:36:36.227 --> 00:36:37.795
saying that everything
I was doing was wrong

00:36:37.795 --> 00:36:39.897
and stupid and reckless
and gonna cause wars.

00:36:39.897 --> 00:36:42.867 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, even as the
announcements were coming out,

00:36:42.867 --> 00:36:44.835 align:start size:88% position:13%
as the deals were coming
out, they were saying

00:36:44.835 --> 00:36:46.304
why these are bad deals.

00:36:46.304 --> 00:36:48.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's like, wait, I
thought that we all wanted

00:36:48.539 --> 00:36:49.373
Middle East peace.

00:36:49.373 --> 00:36:50.775
Wasn't that one thing?

00:36:50.775 --> 00:36:52.610
There's a short list of
things we all agree on.

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:54.545
That used to be one thing
that we all kind of want.

00:36:54.545 --> 00:36:56.480
So did you find that
a lot of the countries

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:01.152
actually were quite willing
once you made that initial,

00:37:01.152 --> 00:37:03.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
once you flipped the
script on them, in essence?

00:37:03.521 --> 00:37:07.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So in some ways,
the entrenchment there

00:37:07.191 --> 00:37:09.260
was just as bad as the
entrenchment at home.

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:11.796
So you think about
all these critics,

00:37:11.796 --> 00:37:15.099 align:start position:0%
and what you have in Washington
is this class of people

00:37:15.099 --> 00:37:16.701
who are basically careerists,

00:37:16.701 --> 00:37:18.669
going in and out of Washington,

00:37:18.669 --> 00:37:21.539
and then you have the
same people who are
writing about them

00:37:21.539 --> 00:37:26.244
are from the think tanks or
the media, and they're also.

00:37:26.244 --> 00:37:27.712
So it's like the same class.

00:37:27.712 --> 00:37:29.213 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I believe this is
what we call the swamp.

00:37:29.213 --> 00:37:30.681
Is this the swamp?

00:37:30.681 --> 00:37:32.383 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and they're all vested
in not being proven wrong.

00:37:32.383 --> 00:37:34.585
So they'd rather you also fail

00:37:34.585 --> 00:37:36.187
than you be proven
right and do it.

00:37:36.187 --> 00:37:38.122
And the whole notion
of the Trump presidency

00:37:38.122 --> 00:37:41.525
was bringing people who are
outsiders to Washington,

00:37:41.525 --> 00:37:43.060
who are results driven.

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:46.397
Again, politicians are about
process and talking points.

00:37:46.397 --> 00:37:48.099
Businessmen are about results.

00:37:48.099 --> 00:37:50.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we come in
with a fundamentally
different approach,

00:37:50.801 --> 00:37:52.470
and that's the theme in my book

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:53.871
that happens time and
time again, right?

00:37:53.871 --> 00:37:55.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's two basic
crosscurrents in the book.

00:37:55.439 --> 00:37:58.676
One is what is was like to
be under endless attack,

00:37:58.676 --> 00:38:00.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a Russia investigation,
an impeachment

00:38:00.811 --> 00:38:03.681
about trying to look into
corruption in Ukraine,

00:38:03.681 --> 00:38:05.650
all the media attacks,
and that's one current.

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:06.851
I call that the waves.

00:38:06.851 --> 00:38:08.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then you have kind
of under the water

00:38:08.286 --> 00:38:09.720
where it's nice and calm,

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:11.689 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is how did all
these policies get done?

00:38:11.689 --> 00:38:12.857
How do we get trade deals done?

00:38:12.857 --> 00:38:14.125
How do we get peace deals done?

00:38:14.125 --> 00:38:15.359
How do we get deals
with Congress done?

00:38:15.359 --> 00:38:16.961
How do we get criminal
justice reform done?

00:38:18.429 --> 00:38:21.565
And so I really try to put
those two things together.

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:24.201
But that complacency
was the same thing

00:38:24.201 --> 00:38:25.436
that I found in the Middle East,

00:38:25.436 --> 00:38:27.371
in the sense that
everyone thought

00:38:27.371 --> 00:38:29.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
that there were limitations
to what they could do.

00:38:29.674 --> 00:38:31.509
And what I found is
all these barriers

00:38:31.509 --> 00:38:32.977
are usually in people's minds.

00:38:32.977 --> 00:38:36.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in the region, everyone
thought it was fixed

00:38:36.814 --> 00:38:38.215
and that if there were
certain things you did,

00:38:38.215 --> 00:38:39.884 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was just unsolvable,
and you had to go

00:38:39.884 --> 00:38:42.286
and solve it the same
way that people before

00:38:42.286 --> 00:38:43.554 align:start size:81% position:19%
had tried to solve
it and failed,

00:38:43.554 --> 00:38:45.222
or else it's gonna
be World War III.

00:38:45.222 --> 00:38:46.524
And I take people
through, again,

00:38:46.524 --> 00:38:49.226
I take them into
the situation room

00:38:49.226 --> 00:38:52.163 align:start size:84% position:16%
and really try to give
them the intensity

00:38:52.163 --> 00:38:53.998
of what it was like to make
some of the first decisions.

00:38:53.998 --> 00:38:56.000 align:start size:97% position:3%
So Trump making the
decision to move the embassy

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.903
to Jerusalem, his Secretary
of State was against it,

00:38:58.903 --> 00:39:00.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
Secretary of Defense
was against it.

00:39:00.438 --> 00:39:02.306
The intel community comes
out with an assessment

00:39:02.306 --> 00:39:05.076
saying that if he does it,
it could be World War III

00:39:05.076 --> 00:39:06.744
and the world's gonna explode.

00:39:06.744 --> 00:39:09.246
- Even though every
president for what.

00:39:09.246 --> 00:39:10.448
- Had said they
were gonna do it.

00:39:10.448 --> 00:39:11.649
- For decades before.

00:39:11.649 --> 00:39:12.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But then they
all got scared away

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:14.085 align:start size:88% position:13%
by the conventional
thinking that happened.

00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:16.287
And what Trump did,
again, a businessman,

00:39:16.287 --> 00:39:18.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
you think about,
okay, that's a risk,

00:39:18.189 --> 00:39:19.457
how do I assess the risk,

00:39:19.457 --> 00:39:20.691
and then how do I
mitigate the risk.

00:39:20.691 --> 00:39:22.993
And I talk about how
we assessed the risk,

00:39:22.993 --> 00:39:24.261
how we mitigated the risk,

00:39:24.261 --> 00:39:26.530
and how we pushed
forward to do it.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:27.732
And at the end of the
day at that point,

00:39:27.732 --> 00:39:29.166 align:start size:88% position:13%
he'd gotten closer with
the Arab countries.

00:39:29.166 --> 00:39:30.534
He said, look, you
can't cherry pick

00:39:30.534 --> 00:39:32.103
your relationship with America.

00:39:32.103 --> 00:39:33.938
Iran's your biggest threat.

00:39:33.938 --> 00:39:35.172
I'm gonna work with you on that

00:39:35.172 --> 00:39:36.540
because I agree
with you on that.

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:38.209
That's in our common interests.

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:40.277 align:start size:97% position:3%
But don't mess around on the
things that I want to do.

00:39:40.277 --> 00:39:41.479
Israel's a sovereign country.

00:39:41.479 --> 00:39:43.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
They have a right to
determine their capital.

00:39:43.347 --> 00:39:44.582 align:start size:81% position:19%
And America's a
sovereign country,

00:39:44.582 --> 00:39:46.183
and we have the right to
recognize the decision

00:39:46.183 --> 00:39:48.686
of another sovereign country,
and that's what I'm doing,

00:39:48.686 --> 00:39:50.721
and I'm not gonna
ask your opinion.

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:53.324 align:start size:97% position:3%
Again, it was very, very
intense because people showed.

00:39:53.324 --> 00:39:55.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
But Trump makes the
decision, and I really use

00:39:55.192 --> 00:39:57.528
some funny stories about
kind of the back and forth

00:39:57.528 --> 00:40:01.265
and the high stakes of it,
but he makes a decision.

00:40:01.265 --> 00:40:03.234
And then we worked
hard to mitigate.

00:40:03.234 --> 00:40:06.036
And then what happens is the
next morning the sun rises,

00:40:06.036 --> 00:40:08.806
and the next evening the sun
sets and the world moves on.

00:40:08.806 --> 00:40:10.241
Same thing with getting
out of the Iran deal.

00:40:10.241 --> 00:40:12.343 align:start size:84% position:16%
People warned him
that if you do this,

00:40:12.343 --> 00:40:14.378 align:start position:0%
you're gonna have World War
II, everything's gonna blow up.

00:40:14.378 --> 00:40:16.814
He makes a decision, the
next morning the sun rises,

00:40:16.814 --> 00:40:18.182
the next evening the sun sets.

00:40:18.182 --> 00:40:19.917
And he figured out
ways to maneuver.

00:40:19.917 --> 00:40:21.752
And so all of a sudden,
everything that seemed

00:40:21.752 --> 00:40:24.121 align:start size:81% position:19%
so fixed to people
all of a sudden

00:40:24.121 --> 00:40:28.826
was becoming a little bit
more of a fluid situation.

00:40:28.826 --> 00:40:31.195 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so the big obstacle
was the Israelis

00:40:31.195 --> 00:40:32.463
and the Palestinians.

00:40:32.463 --> 00:40:34.432 align:start size:81% position:19%
Again, as I looked
at that issue,

00:40:34.432 --> 00:40:36.434
and I go through
this in the book,

00:40:36.434 --> 00:40:39.603 align:start size:84% position:16%
that seemed to me
like a fake situation.

00:40:39.603 --> 00:40:42.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
It'd been one of
these manufactured
situations for years,

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:46.110
and there was just so much
assymetry and incentives.

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:47.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Palestinian leadership
was getting rich.

00:40:47.711 --> 00:40:49.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, Bibi Netanyahu,
who runs a military

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:52.716
and economic superpower in
Israel, would fly to Israel,

00:40:52.716 --> 00:40:54.752
he'd fly in an El
Al commercial jet.

00:40:54.752 --> 00:40:58.456
Abbas, who I'm sitting with
him in a meeting like we are,

00:40:58.456 --> 00:41:00.191 align:start size:97% position:3%
he wants a cigarette, he puts
a cigarette in his mouth,

00:41:00.191 --> 00:41:02.960
somebody comes over and lights
it for him like he's a king,

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:04.762
and then he flies to Washington

00:41:04.762 --> 00:41:07.097
in a $60 million BBJ,
you know what I mean?

00:41:07.097 --> 00:41:10.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like the whole
thing was he was rich,

00:41:10.301 --> 00:41:12.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
his family was rich,
everybody was doing great,

00:41:12.369 --> 00:41:15.940
but the Palestinian people
weren't getting the love.

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:20.945
So we exposed kind of what
a bunch of crap that was

00:41:22.379 --> 00:41:23.814
and how, again, the goal
of a leader should be to,

00:41:23.814 --> 00:41:25.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
number one, provide
security for their people,

00:41:25.349 --> 00:41:27.218
number two, to provide
economic opportunities

00:41:27.218 --> 00:41:29.253 align:start size:81% position:19%
so people can have
a better life.

00:41:29.253 --> 00:41:31.088
Security, he did
as much for himself

00:41:31.088 --> 00:41:32.890
as he did for the people,
and I have some examples

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:35.025 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the book of ways
that we tested,

00:41:35.025 --> 00:41:36.961
and he kind of was bluffing,
and then we'd call his bluff

00:41:36.961 --> 00:41:39.230
and he backs off very weakly.

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:41.365
And then we also have
situations in there

00:41:41.365 --> 00:41:44.335
with economics, where we expose

00:41:44.335 --> 00:41:45.970
that he wasn't really
trying to do that.

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:49.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that was kind of
what we have in there,

00:41:49.173 --> 00:41:51.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that also allowed the
leaders to think differently

00:41:51.775 --> 00:41:53.277
than they did before.

00:41:53.277 --> 00:41:54.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How much more do you think
you could have got done

00:41:54.678 --> 00:41:56.113
had there been four more years?

00:41:56.113 --> 00:41:57.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
Were dominoes really
ready to fall?

00:41:57.681 --> 00:42:00.251
I remember right before the
election, people kept saying,

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:02.086
oh no, there's six
more deals in place

00:42:02.086 --> 00:42:03.554
and there's all
this other stuff.

00:42:03.554 --> 00:42:05.256
- We wouldn't have even
needed four more years.

00:42:05.256 --> 00:42:06.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
We would have needed
six more months.

00:42:06.824 --> 00:42:07.858
And again, I outlined this all

00:42:07.858 --> 00:42:09.460
for the incoming administration.

00:42:10.628 --> 00:42:12.096
So the last deal
that we got done,

00:42:12.096 --> 00:42:13.597
and it was a high wire deal,

00:42:13.597 --> 00:42:16.166
and I have some very crazy
moments of this in my book,

00:42:16.166 --> 00:42:19.937
was after we get
UAE and Bahrain,

00:42:19.937 --> 00:42:23.274
you have Sudan and
Morocco, Sudan goes.

00:42:23.274 --> 00:42:25.109
Then Morocco we get in
the lame duck period,

00:42:25.109 --> 00:42:26.710
which people were surprised,

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:28.479
and we were trying
to get Saudi to go,

00:42:28.479 --> 00:42:30.481 align:start size:81% position:19%
but we couldn't
get Saudi or Qatar

00:42:30.481 --> 00:42:32.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
until the rift was
resolved between them.

00:42:32.283 --> 00:42:34.084 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I'd been working on that
for about three months,

00:42:34.084 --> 00:42:35.686
and we had amazing breakthroughs

00:42:35.686 --> 00:42:38.756 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a very intense
negotiation, and now, again,

00:42:38.756 --> 00:42:41.759
literally on the
tarmac to take off,

00:42:41.759 --> 00:42:44.128
and the deal dies, and
it dies several times.

00:42:44.128 --> 00:42:45.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
These are complicated
situations.

00:42:45.696 --> 00:42:47.932
So that one we got done Jan 5th,

00:42:47.932 --> 00:42:50.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I think opened it
up for the administration.

00:42:50.267 --> 00:42:51.669
I laid out for them a road map.

00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:53.771
We were close with
Mauritania, I felt.

00:42:53.771 --> 00:42:55.239
I think Saudi was very doable.

00:42:55.239 --> 00:42:57.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think now it's a
question of when, not if.

00:42:57.708 --> 00:43:00.511 align:start size:97% position:3%
But it needs U.S. leadership
to make it happen.

00:43:00.511 --> 00:43:03.714
And so what I basically said
to the incoming administration

00:43:03.714 --> 00:43:06.317
is look, congratulations,
you guys are gonna get

00:43:06.317 --> 00:43:07.484
all these deals done.

00:43:07.484 --> 00:43:09.486 align:start size:84% position:16%
We just were running
out of clock.

00:43:09.486 --> 00:43:10.988
But just don't run to Iran.

00:43:10.988 --> 00:43:12.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you run to Iran, you're
gonna upset everything.

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:14.892 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have Iran weaker
than they've ever been.

00:43:14.892 --> 00:43:16.393
Our policy's working.

00:43:16.393 --> 00:43:18.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
Just tell Iran you're too busy
working with climate change

00:43:18.395 --> 00:43:21.098
and COVID and all this
stuff you're focused on,

00:43:21.098 --> 00:43:23.934
and wait for them
to come to you.

00:43:23.934 --> 00:43:26.604 align:start size:88% position:13%
What I would do is go
to Saudi Arabia and say,

00:43:26.604 --> 00:43:28.739
look, we want you to
do a deal with Israel.

00:43:28.739 --> 00:43:30.641 align:start size:84% position:16%
We'll wait six months,
we'll wait a year.

00:43:30.641 --> 00:43:32.076
Let's work on a plan together.

00:43:32.076 --> 00:43:33.711
I know we said some bad
stuff in the campaign.

00:43:33.711 --> 00:43:36.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've been a country
that's worked with America

00:43:36.246 --> 00:43:40.084
for 80 years, you've been a
great ally in many regards.

00:43:40.084 --> 00:43:41.585
What's it gonna take
to get that to happen

00:43:41.585 --> 00:43:43.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
so we can finally
end, once and for all,

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:44.855
the Arab Israeli conflict?

00:43:46.023 --> 00:43:49.593
And they nodded and then
did absolutely nothing

00:43:49.593 --> 00:43:51.261
according to that.

00:43:51.261 --> 00:43:53.597
- Did you literally
hand them a packet?

00:43:53.597 --> 00:43:56.233
Did you hand them, this
is what's got to happen?

00:43:56.233 --> 00:43:58.869 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I didn't write a memo,
but I sat down twice

00:43:58.869 --> 00:44:00.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the incoming
national security adviser

00:44:00.137 --> 00:44:01.372
and basically outlined.

00:44:01.372 --> 00:44:02.773
I said look, this
is where we are.

00:44:02.773 --> 00:44:05.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was giving full transparency
into the discussions.

00:44:06.543 --> 00:44:10.414 align:start position:0%
I fundamentally believe it
could have happened very quickly

00:44:10.414 --> 00:44:11.882
had they taken the policy.

00:44:11.882 --> 00:44:13.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
But again, they ran to Iran,
got down on their knees,

00:44:13.717 --> 00:44:14.985
started begging them.

00:44:14.985 --> 00:44:17.921
Trump used to always say
Iran's never won a war,

00:44:17.921 --> 00:44:20.090
but never lost a negotiation.

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:22.026
And what they did was terrible.

00:44:22.026 --> 00:44:23.927
Then they started kind
of brow-beating Saudi

00:44:23.927 --> 00:44:26.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
which made them say, okay,
there's other countries

00:44:26.997 --> 00:44:28.165
in the world too.

00:44:28.165 --> 00:44:29.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then they're running
over to China.

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.136
They alienated a lot
of the people there.

00:44:32.136 --> 00:44:34.471
Look, I think their recent
policy has shifted back.

00:44:34.471 --> 00:44:36.273
They went to Saudi Arabia.

00:44:36.273 --> 00:44:39.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
What they did with
UAE, they had missiles

00:44:39.476 --> 00:44:43.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
shot at their airport
from the Houthis in Yemen,

00:44:43.947 --> 00:44:46.316
so we actually designated the
Houthis a terror organization

00:44:46.316 --> 00:44:48.318 align:start position:0%
because they were firing
missiles into two of our allies

00:44:48.318 --> 00:44:50.721
in Saudi Arabia and
UAE and chanting

00:44:50.721 --> 00:44:53.390
insignificant statements
like death to America,

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:54.892
death to Israel.

00:44:54.892 --> 00:44:56.794
And the first thing the
Biden administration does

00:44:56.794 --> 00:44:58.829 align:start position:0%
when they come in is they
undesignate them as terrorists.

00:44:58.829 --> 00:45:01.265
And then they fire
missiles into UAE

00:45:01.265 --> 00:45:02.966
that land at their airport.

00:45:02.966 --> 00:45:06.003
That's like the nerve
center of commerce,

00:45:06.003 --> 00:45:07.337
and the U.S. doesn't call,

00:45:07.337 --> 00:45:08.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're not restocking
their weapons.

00:45:08.505 --> 00:45:12.242
It was a very, very tense time.

00:45:12.242 --> 00:45:14.344
So I think right now,
to give them credit,

00:45:14.344 --> 00:45:17.247
I think they are understanding

00:45:17.247 --> 00:45:19.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
that maybe they didn't
handle it as well

00:45:19.016 --> 00:45:20.284
as they could have
in the beginning,

00:45:20.284 --> 00:45:21.819
and it does feel, just
from what I'm seeing,

00:45:21.819 --> 00:45:25.489
I'm an outsider now, from the
inside they're trying harder,

00:45:25.489 --> 00:45:28.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I still think that these
deals are very gettable

00:45:28.125 --> 00:45:29.693
if they focus on it
in the right way.

00:45:29.693 --> 00:45:31.595
- Yeah, do you think
part of the problem is

00:45:31.595 --> 00:45:33.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
they don't want to do
anything because it'll feel

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:35.499 align:start size:97% position:3%
like it's just an extension
of what you guys did,

00:45:35.499 --> 00:45:37.835
and they sort of want to
erase everything, right?

00:45:37.835 --> 00:45:39.536
They don't want them referred
to as the Abraham Accords.

00:45:39.536 --> 00:45:42.005
- No, this is one thing
they've come around on,

00:45:42.005 --> 00:45:44.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
and actually they participate
in some of the events

00:45:44.541 --> 00:45:46.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
we've done to try to
promote the Abraham Accords.

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:47.711
It took them a year, right?

00:45:47.711 --> 00:45:48.979 align:start size:97% position:3%
The first year they wouldn't
call it by its name,

00:45:48.979 --> 00:45:51.315
but I think that that
was high level people.

00:45:51.315 --> 00:45:54.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think a lot of the career
folks in the State Department,

00:45:54.785 --> 00:45:56.386
they were amazingly
helpful in getting

00:45:56.386 --> 00:45:58.522
the Abraham Accords
done and implemented.

00:45:58.522 --> 00:46:01.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think even dealing
with Brett McGurk,

00:46:01.258 --> 00:46:02.493
he was very supportive.

00:46:02.493 --> 00:46:04.828
So I think there are
a lot of people there

00:46:04.828 --> 00:46:07.498
who want to see it happen,

00:46:07.498 --> 00:46:09.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think with the right
prioritization it can.

00:46:09.266 --> 00:46:11.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just, again, I don't
know if these guys

00:46:11.668 --> 00:46:13.570
have the right ability
to do the negotiation.

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:15.372
Again, they're career
politicians, right?

00:46:15.372 --> 00:46:16.707
They're diplomats.

00:46:16.707 --> 00:46:18.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were the anti-diplomats
in the sense

00:46:18.809 --> 00:46:21.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we basically came
in and we sat down

00:46:21.678 --> 00:46:23.180
and we figured out
how to make deals.

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:25.916
We got six peace deals done,
we got major trade deals done.

00:46:25.916 --> 00:46:27.985
And again, I write
about how we did it,

00:46:27.985 --> 00:46:29.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
which again, every
single thing in this book

00:46:29.753 --> 00:46:32.756
that ended up being effective
towards getting deals done

00:46:32.756 --> 00:46:34.258
are things that I
got criticized for

00:46:34.258 --> 00:46:36.093
in the pages of the New York
Times and the Washington Post.

00:46:36.093 --> 00:46:37.628
- Of course, of course.

00:46:37.628 --> 00:46:39.329
So let's talk a little bit
about the COVID situation

00:46:39.329 --> 00:46:41.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then sort of how
policy worked around that,

00:46:41.165 --> 00:46:43.867
because one of the things
that kind of blew me away

00:46:43.867 --> 00:46:45.702
the entire time was
that the same people

00:46:45.702 --> 00:46:47.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
who every day were calling
Donald Trump Hitler

00:46:47.771 --> 00:46:49.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
and all the worst
things in the world,

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:51.708
he's a dictator,
blah, blah, blah,

00:46:51.708 --> 00:46:53.277
they were the same
people who wanted him

00:46:53.277 --> 00:46:55.546 align:start size:97% position:3%
to have all the federal power
to do whatever he wanted,

00:46:55.546 --> 00:46:57.514 align:start size:81% position:19%
because very early
on he was like,

00:46:57.514 --> 00:47:00.083
let's leave it to the states
and let them figure it out.

00:47:00.083 --> 00:47:01.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
You talk a little bit
about some of the tensions

00:47:01.785 --> 00:47:03.320
with Fauci and all that.

00:47:03.320 --> 00:47:05.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
How crazy was
that, to advise him

00:47:05.923 --> 00:47:10.127
in this sort of unknown moment?

00:47:10.127 --> 00:47:13.597
- So that was by far one
of the scariest moments

00:47:13.597 --> 00:47:14.832
you could ever have, right?

00:47:14.832 --> 00:47:18.101
And again, this was
after three years,

00:47:18.101 --> 00:47:19.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
and by the way,
hypocrisy, I figured out,

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:21.638 align:start position:0%
is not a new thing in
Washington after like day three.

00:47:21.638 --> 00:47:23.473
It didn't take me
after three years.

00:47:24.975 --> 00:47:27.477
But that was by
far the scariest.

00:47:27.477 --> 00:47:28.879
We had things really moving.

00:47:29.746 --> 00:47:31.315
Again, our economy was roaring,

00:47:31.315 --> 00:47:33.650
we had great people in
place, we had plans,

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:35.385
we were building
for a second term.

00:47:35.385 --> 00:47:37.020
Then COVID comes out of nowhere

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:40.123 align:start size:88% position:13%
and really hit the world
like a ton of bricks.

00:47:40.123 --> 00:47:43.093
And I write about in
the book my experience

00:47:43.093 --> 00:47:45.162
getting pulled in, again,
not as a medical expert,

00:47:45.162 --> 00:47:46.964
but just as somebody
to help operationally

00:47:46.964 --> 00:47:48.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
who knew how to move the
levers of government.

00:47:48.932 --> 00:47:51.568
Vice President Pence, who
I worked very closely with,

00:47:51.568 --> 00:47:54.271
asked me to come in and help him

00:47:54.271 --> 00:47:57.741
execute with certain things,
like scale testing and whatnot.

00:47:57.741 --> 00:47:59.009
But it was very interesting.

00:47:59.009 --> 00:48:01.144
So you're making a
lot of decisions.

00:48:01.144 --> 00:48:03.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
The media was the first thing
they got Trump on, right,

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:05.616 align:start position:0%
because if he was more careful,
then he was killing jobs.

00:48:05.616 --> 00:48:07.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
If he was opening the economy,
he was killing people.

00:48:07.784 --> 00:48:11.054
So CNN kept those tickers
up, depending on the day,

00:48:11.054 --> 00:48:12.456
and they were loving the fact

00:48:12.456 --> 00:48:13.657 align:start size:81% position:19%
that they could
just bludgeon him.

00:48:13.657 --> 00:48:14.925 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you could
always find a nurse

00:48:14.925 --> 00:48:16.727
who was afraid they were
gonna run out of stuff.

00:48:16.727 --> 00:48:18.061
We were playing whack-a-mole.

00:48:18.061 --> 00:48:19.196
I mean, I had stories
that I write about

00:48:19.196 --> 00:48:21.131
where I'd see a nurse on CNN

00:48:21.131 --> 00:48:22.733
complaining they don't
have masks at a hospital.

00:48:22.733 --> 00:48:23.667
I'd get on the phone with
the head of the hospital

00:48:23.667 --> 00:48:25.602
and say, what do you need?

00:48:25.602 --> 00:48:26.803
We'll get it to you.

00:48:26.803 --> 00:48:28.639
And we made sure
all these shortages

00:48:28.639 --> 00:48:31.875
that everyone was fearing were
gonna happen did not happen,

00:48:31.875 --> 00:48:33.677
and a lot of it was because we
thought like business people,

00:48:33.677 --> 00:48:35.145
not like bureaucrats, right?

00:48:35.145 --> 00:48:37.748
So I tell stories about how
we find all these masks,

00:48:37.748 --> 00:48:39.983
we figure out how to
do DPAs to get them.

00:48:39.983 --> 00:48:42.286
In order to get them over,
we don't have enough planes

00:48:42.286 --> 00:48:43.720
as the U.S., so I
call Fred Smith,

00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:46.657
I call David Abney
from UPS and Fed Ex,

00:48:46.657 --> 00:48:47.824
and I say, look, we
need your planes.

00:48:47.824 --> 00:48:49.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
And they're like,
they're your air force.

00:48:49.359 --> 00:48:50.594
Take them, anything you do.

00:48:50.594 --> 00:48:52.963
And even David Abney
only months later,

00:48:52.963 --> 00:48:54.531 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've never seen anything
like it in my career,

00:48:54.531 --> 00:48:56.099
as quickly as I could
land planes in Asia,

00:48:56.099 --> 00:48:57.634 align:start size:88% position:13%
you had them filled with
masks and sent back.

00:48:57.634 --> 00:49:00.437 align:start size:97% position:3%
We were under a week away
from running out of supplies

00:49:00.437 --> 00:49:03.507
in this country, and again,
Trump thought out of the box.

00:49:03.507 --> 00:49:05.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
He empowered business
people to do it

00:49:05.575 --> 00:49:07.477
and got a lot of things done.

00:49:07.477 --> 00:49:08.845
And ultimately the
biggest test of that

00:49:08.845 --> 00:49:10.213
was Operation Warp Speed, right?

00:49:10.213 --> 00:49:14.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
So even Fauci said Trump
is over-exaggerating.

00:49:14.451 --> 00:49:16.053 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's gonna be at least
a year and a half

00:49:16.053 --> 00:49:17.454
until we get a working vaccine.

00:49:17.454 --> 00:49:20.290 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so we got the
bureaucracy out of the way,

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:23.794 align:start position:0%
we brought in the private
sector, brought in the military.

00:49:23.794 --> 00:49:25.329
Each thing, and this
is one of the things

00:49:25.329 --> 00:49:27.497
I think readers have really
like about the book so far,

00:49:27.497 --> 00:49:29.666
is that I'm pretty honest
about mistakes that I made

00:49:29.666 --> 00:49:31.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
and things that I
could have done better,

00:49:31.768 --> 00:49:33.236
but I show from all these things

00:49:33.236 --> 00:49:35.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
how I learned from these
experiences that enabled us.

00:49:35.405 --> 00:49:37.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the lessons we learned
on scaling testing

00:49:37.908 --> 00:49:40.143
was that I always say
to solve a problem,

00:49:40.143 --> 00:49:42.346
there's three things you need.

00:49:42.346 --> 00:49:45.849
You need imagination, you need
money, and you need gravity.

00:49:45.849 --> 00:49:47.684 align:start size:88% position:13%
Those three are
basically the catch alls

00:49:47.684 --> 00:49:49.319
of the categories you need.

00:49:49.319 --> 00:49:51.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
For testing, we had, I
think, a really good plan,

00:49:51.822 --> 00:49:53.390
so the imagination check.

00:49:53.390 --> 00:49:54.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're the federal
government, you print money,

00:49:54.825 --> 00:49:57.794
so we had endless cash,
that wasn't our issue.

00:49:57.794 --> 00:49:59.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we just couldn't
produce q-tips,

00:49:59.629 --> 00:50:01.031
basically swabs for people.

00:50:01.031 --> 00:50:02.733
We're about to launch 400 sites,

00:50:02.733 --> 00:50:03.967
and they come to
us and say, sorry,

00:50:03.967 --> 00:50:05.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
we only have 1.2 million
swabs in the country.

00:50:05.402 --> 00:50:08.071
So I'm like, man, and
I started learning

00:50:08.071 --> 00:50:09.873 align:start size:84% position:16%
the lowest cost item
was gonna become

00:50:09.873 --> 00:50:11.641
our biggest bottleneck.

00:50:11.641 --> 00:50:14.111
And that was just
a gravity issue,

00:50:14.111 --> 00:50:15.345
so we worked that through.

00:50:15.345 --> 00:50:16.980
That was actually one of
our issues with Fauci,

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:19.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
because he goes on
television and he says,

00:50:19.383 --> 00:50:21.685
oh, testing, we're
just not there yet,

00:50:21.685 --> 00:50:22.953
instead of saying like, dude,

00:50:22.953 --> 00:50:24.688
you are in charge of
infectious diseases.

00:50:24.688 --> 00:50:26.723
You didn't tell us that we
didn't have enough supplies

00:50:26.723 --> 00:50:29.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
in case one came, that you're
sitting on the task force

00:50:29.026 --> 00:50:31.128
every day, you've given
us no additional ideas

00:50:31.128 --> 00:50:32.629
for how to do this better.

00:50:32.629 --> 00:50:34.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you're on TV,
explain we have a plan,

00:50:34.331 --> 00:50:36.333
we're doing this as fast as
we can, we're going fast.

00:50:36.333 --> 00:50:38.168 align:start size:84% position:16%
Instead it was like
are you a sportscaster

00:50:38.168 --> 00:50:39.136
or are you a member
of the task force?

00:50:39.136 --> 00:50:40.404
- So what do you make of him,

00:50:40.404 --> 00:50:41.371
or what did you
make of him then,

00:50:41.371 --> 00:50:43.073
and is that different than now?

00:50:43.073 --> 00:50:44.608
- I'll get to that in a
sec, but just to finish

00:50:44.608 --> 00:50:48.345
what I was saying is that
ultimately those lessons

00:50:48.345 --> 00:50:50.447
we learned from Warp Speed,

00:50:50.447 --> 00:50:52.215
which is that we did
everything right early,

00:50:52.215 --> 00:50:53.817
and that enabled
us to be successful

00:50:53.817 --> 00:50:56.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
and ramp up the
manufacturing simultaneously.

00:50:56.019 --> 00:50:58.021
And I give some lessons on that.

00:50:58.021 --> 00:51:01.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
With him, I think
that to make it for,

00:51:01.491 --> 00:51:03.960
first of all, nobody should
be in a job for 50 years,

00:51:03.960 --> 00:51:05.529
especially not in
a government job.

00:51:05.529 --> 00:51:07.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think he was an
excellent politician

00:51:08.732 --> 00:51:10.534 align:start size:88% position:13%
in terms of his ability
to be a survivor,

00:51:10.534 --> 00:51:15.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think he also
embraced a lot of the,

00:51:15.005 --> 00:51:17.474
people would always
build these people up

00:51:17.474 --> 00:51:18.942
as a counter to Trump.

00:51:18.942 --> 00:51:21.845 align:start position:0%
Like take Michael Avenatti,
who is now, I think, in prison.

00:51:23.246 --> 00:51:26.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
The media made him a hero
because he was against Trump.

00:51:26.416 --> 00:51:29.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the media would create
these false heroes,

00:51:29.186 --> 00:51:31.154 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think with Fauci,
they elevated him

00:51:31.154 --> 00:51:33.890
like they elevated
20 people before.

00:51:33.890 --> 00:51:35.859
And I think he embraced it,
and I think he liked it,

00:51:35.859 --> 00:51:38.261 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think that
that led to a lot of,

00:51:38.261 --> 00:51:41.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
it wasn't about
service, and it led to

00:51:41.198 --> 00:51:43.500 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of misinformation
and confusion

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:44.868
that didn't have to be the case.

00:51:44.868 --> 00:51:47.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, again, I
give one great scene

00:51:47.471 --> 00:51:49.039 align:start size:88% position:13%
where Trump is fighting
with him saying,

00:51:49.039 --> 00:51:51.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
Doc, I'm not gonna keep
the country closed.

00:51:51.374 --> 00:51:54.044 align:start position:0%
I'm not gonna oversee the
funeral of the greatest country

00:51:54.044 --> 00:51:55.745
and economy in the
history of the world.

00:51:55.745 --> 00:51:57.214 align:start size:81% position:19%
And he meekly goes
back and says,

00:51:57.214 --> 00:51:58.448
look, I'm just here to
give medical advice.

00:51:58.448 --> 00:51:59.282
I'm just a doctor.

00:51:59.282 --> 00:52:00.750
You're the president.

00:52:00.750 --> 00:52:02.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your job is to account
for all the other factors.

00:52:02.519 --> 00:52:03.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he backed off
pretty quickly there.

00:52:03.987 --> 00:52:06.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so again, interesting
guy to work with.

00:52:06.289 --> 00:52:07.657
He was very pleasant.

00:52:07.657 --> 00:52:10.260
We had a good working
relationship together,

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:12.596
but very politically
calculating and savvy,

00:52:12.596 --> 00:52:15.432
and wasn't always on our team.

00:52:15.432 --> 00:52:17.701
- Did you feel that from
the agencies in general,

00:52:17.701 --> 00:52:20.203
that the CDC or the NIH or
whoever else you worked with,

00:52:20.203 --> 00:52:22.139 align:start size:88% position:13%
that generally they
weren't gonna be staffed

00:52:22.139 --> 00:52:26.710 align:start position:0%
with Trump people, so there
was an odd incentive structure?

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:29.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
They knew an
election was coming.

00:52:29.479 --> 00:52:30.714
- Yes and no.

00:52:30.714 --> 00:52:32.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
Look, so Dr. Collins
at NIH, I thought

00:52:32.916 --> 00:52:35.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
was very professional,
very knowledgeable,

00:52:35.352 --> 00:52:36.620
always doing well.

00:52:36.620 --> 00:52:38.221
We had a couple of
tensions with him

00:52:38.221 --> 00:52:41.458 align:start size:88% position:13%
about getting EUAs,
because they were trying

00:52:41.458 --> 00:52:42.926
to preserve perfect science.

00:52:42.926 --> 00:52:46.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we're saying, like
saying convalescent plasma

00:52:46.129 --> 00:52:48.031
was an issue where we
got killed in the paper

00:52:48.031 --> 00:52:49.633
because we were pushing to say

00:52:49.633 --> 00:52:51.968
this is something that's
been around for 100 years.

00:52:51.968 --> 00:52:53.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have a study that's
showing that it's working.

00:52:53.837 --> 00:52:55.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
Get it out there, let
doctors make the decision

00:52:55.872 --> 00:52:58.074
if they want it or not, but
they're saying the study,

00:52:58.074 --> 00:52:59.643
which was done by the
Mayo Clinic, I think,

00:52:59.643 --> 00:53:01.978
or Cleveland Clinic,
wasn't done pristinely.

00:53:01.978 --> 00:53:03.547
I said, look, this is war time.

00:53:03.547 --> 00:53:05.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
If it's looking good, let
people make their decisions.

00:53:05.749 --> 00:53:07.250
It's not gonna kill people.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:08.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that was where we had some
fights with the scientists.

00:53:08.952 --> 00:53:13.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
The CDC was an absolute
barn fire mess.

00:53:15.125 --> 00:53:17.694
There was one fight I
had with them where,

00:53:17.694 --> 00:53:19.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
and again, I backed off
quickly because I'm saying,

00:53:19.262 --> 00:53:20.697
okay, how is this gonna
look on the front page

00:53:20.697 --> 00:53:21.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the New York Times,
and I'm screaming

00:53:21.998 --> 00:53:23.700
at the head of the CDC.

00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:26.703
But they basically were
saying this 14 days rule,

00:53:26.703 --> 00:53:27.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I said, okay, they're
telling our people

00:53:27.971 --> 00:53:30.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
they have to
quarantine for 14 days.

00:53:30.807 --> 00:53:32.409 align:start size:81% position:19%
I said, where does
that come from?

00:53:32.409 --> 00:53:33.577
Well, we have a study.

00:53:33.577 --> 00:53:35.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
She said, I'll
bring you the study.

00:53:35.078 --> 00:53:36.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that night, again, I
was on two hours of sleep,

00:53:36.012 --> 00:53:37.681
but I read through the study.

00:53:37.681 --> 00:53:41.551
And I'm seeing that it's
like a study of 182 people,

00:53:41.551 --> 00:53:44.854
of which they know 140
of them got the disease.

00:53:44.854 --> 00:53:48.425
The average time from
contracting the disease

00:53:48.425 --> 00:53:51.027 align:start size:84% position:16%
to showing symptoms
was 5.1 days,

00:53:51.027 --> 00:53:53.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
and because the sample
size was so small,

00:53:53.129 --> 00:53:54.598 align:start size:84% position:16%
they used two
standard deviations

00:53:54.598 --> 00:53:56.132 align:start size:88% position:13%
and told the whole
world they had to spend

00:53:56.132 --> 00:53:57.701
14 days in quarantine.

00:53:57.701 --> 00:53:58.969
- [Dave] Wow.

00:53:58.969 --> 00:54:00.503
- I'm saying, wait,
we're screwing up

00:54:00.503 --> 00:54:05.141
the entire global economy off
the sample size of 140 people.

00:54:05.141 --> 00:54:07.244
So my point is like
a lot of the things

00:54:07.244 --> 00:54:10.180
they were saying in the name
of science were not scientific.

00:54:10.180 --> 00:54:12.082
You know, we tried to be nimble,

00:54:12.082 --> 00:54:13.883 align:start size:88% position:13%
and at first we trusted
all these people

00:54:13.883 --> 00:54:15.118
because what the
hell do we know?

00:54:15.118 --> 00:54:17.487
These were the experts
on infectious diseases.

00:54:17.487 --> 00:54:21.891 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I think over time
we became more convinced

00:54:21.891 --> 00:54:23.827 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we had to be pragmatic
about these things

00:54:23.827 --> 00:54:26.296
and take their guidance
and their perspective,

00:54:26.296 --> 00:54:27.998
but weigh it accordingly,
knowing that they were

00:54:27.998 --> 00:54:32.102
a lot of academics who
weren't very operational.

00:54:32.102 --> 00:54:34.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
Somebody like Dr. Birx
was actually quite helpful

00:54:34.037 --> 00:54:36.339
because she'd been
on the front lines

00:54:36.339 --> 00:54:38.008 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a lot of these different
countries in Africa.

00:54:38.008 --> 00:54:41.344 align:start size:97% position:3%
So when it came to problems,
she knew operationally

00:54:41.344 --> 00:54:42.579
how to solve them, and again,

00:54:42.579 --> 00:54:43.913
I thought she was a
very useful voice.

00:54:43.913 --> 00:54:45.348
I learned a lot from her.

00:54:45.348 --> 00:54:49.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it was a very
complicated time to be in.

00:54:49.519 --> 00:54:51.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
An amazing
experience, and again,

00:54:51.921 --> 00:54:54.357 align:start size:88% position:13%
I do think the work
that President Trump did

00:54:54.357 --> 00:54:56.726
saved a ton of lives, and I
think if you would have had

00:54:56.726 --> 00:54:59.496
a normal politician there
instead of an outsider,

00:54:59.496 --> 00:55:01.831
somebody who had a
results mentality,

00:55:01.831 --> 00:55:04.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
the outcomes would have been
significantly different.

00:55:04.367 --> 00:55:05.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well maybe this
is a silly question,

00:55:05.635 --> 00:55:10.473
but do you think, had
the Democrats, media,

00:55:11.775 --> 00:55:13.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of the organizations,
CDC, et cetera,

00:55:13.176 --> 00:55:14.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
had they all been willing
to work with Trump

00:55:14.544 --> 00:55:16.546
a little bit more, do
you think we could be

00:55:16.546 --> 00:55:18.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
in some real golden
age right now,

00:55:18.281 --> 00:55:21.084
that so many of the things
that we see with the economy,

00:55:21.084 --> 00:55:23.286
supply chain, just all the
nonsense that we're seeing,

00:55:23.286 --> 00:55:26.122
disaster in Afghanistan,
that we really did have

00:55:26.122 --> 00:55:28.692
a chance to fix so much?

00:55:28.692 --> 00:55:31.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
You mentioned before
the peace deals,

00:55:31.461 --> 00:55:33.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
there was a whole bunch
coming right there.

00:55:33.229 --> 00:55:35.098
Do you think that had
he, he was willing

00:55:35.098 --> 00:55:36.833
to work with people, right?

00:55:36.833 --> 00:55:38.101
- Very much so.

00:55:38.101 --> 00:55:39.235
Look, I think that
one of the things

00:55:39.235 --> 00:55:41.705 align:start size:88% position:13%
I try to be very
honest about in my book

00:55:41.705 --> 00:55:43.840 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that I had certain
biases coming in,

00:55:43.840 --> 00:55:45.308
and my biases changed.

00:55:45.308 --> 00:55:46.576
But we all have biases.

00:55:46.576 --> 00:55:49.479
I think that the media
today, in particular,

00:55:49.479 --> 00:55:51.481
is not really honest
about their biases,

00:55:51.481 --> 00:55:54.317 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I don't think
people are as liberated,

00:55:54.317 --> 00:55:57.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
where they still feel like
playing to the standard

00:55:57.420 --> 00:55:59.956
of the media is the
appropriate standard

00:55:59.956 --> 00:56:02.926
by which to try to
conduct your conduct.

00:56:02.926 --> 00:56:04.661 align:start size:88% position:13%
And ultimately we became
liberated and said,

00:56:04.661 --> 00:56:07.364 align:start size:88% position:13%
okay, let's just do
what we think is right.

00:56:07.364 --> 00:56:09.265 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I do think a lot of these
people in these agencies

00:56:09.265 --> 00:56:11.868
tried to play to the media,
and the media was rewarding

00:56:11.868 --> 00:56:13.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
anyone who was in
opposition to Trump.

00:56:13.370 --> 00:56:16.072
So that was kind of the
general playing field.

00:56:16.072 --> 00:56:17.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
Again, not right or wrong,
it's just the reality

00:56:17.774 --> 00:56:19.275
of what it was.

00:56:19.275 --> 00:56:21.378
I think from a pure policy
point of view, like I said,

00:56:21.378 --> 00:56:24.314 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think I'm more of a
centrist, more of a pragmatist

00:56:24.314 --> 00:56:26.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
in terms of how I think
about these things.

00:56:26.483 --> 00:56:29.018 align:start position:0%
A lot of President Trump's
policies I thought were spot on.

00:56:29.018 --> 00:56:31.321
Like trade, there was
a big debate on trade.

00:56:31.321 --> 00:56:33.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
Bringing our manufacturing
jobs back to America

00:56:33.323 --> 00:56:35.759
was something that was anti,

00:56:35.759 --> 00:56:38.094
I mean, Paul Ryan
was pissed at us

00:56:38.094 --> 00:56:40.397
and saying you're never gonna
get a USMCA trade deal done.

00:56:40.397 --> 00:56:42.132
But my mentor on that
was Bob Lighthizer who,

00:56:42.132 --> 00:56:44.434
by the way, is I think the
best character in this book.

00:56:44.434 --> 00:56:46.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
People have come away
saying that they really want

00:56:46.703 --> 00:56:49.239
to meet Bob, and he really was
a mentor and a great friend,

00:56:49.239 --> 00:56:51.274
and hysterically funny.

00:56:51.274 --> 00:56:54.844
And I write about what it
was like working with him.

00:56:54.844 --> 00:56:56.513
But trade was an issue.

00:56:56.513 --> 00:56:57.781
You know, immigration, right?

00:56:57.781 --> 00:56:59.749
So a lot of my friends
in New York would say,

00:56:59.749 --> 00:57:01.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, the wall is
xenophobic and racist.

00:57:01.718 --> 00:57:04.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I'd say, okay, well
maybe that's what they feel,

00:57:04.154 --> 00:57:05.422
but then you get down
there and you're like,

00:57:05.422 --> 00:57:06.656
well, it's just like
a sovereignty issue.

00:57:06.656 --> 00:57:08.992
And then I talked to
the border agents.

00:57:08.992 --> 00:57:11.294
Every mile of wall you put
up, we can do more and more.

00:57:11.294 --> 00:57:14.164
And I write about actually
how we got the wall built,

00:57:14.164 --> 00:57:16.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
because that became a
symbol of Trump's success.

00:57:16.599 --> 00:57:19.669
And so the Democrats, and
even, I mean, Steve Bannon,

00:57:19.669 --> 00:57:21.271
John Kelly, they were
supposed to get it done

00:57:21.271 --> 00:57:22.338
for the first two years.

00:57:22.338 --> 00:57:23.273
Both failed.

00:57:23.273 --> 00:57:24.507
Trump asked me to get it done.

00:57:24.507 --> 00:57:26.509
I write about how we
found the $11 billion

00:57:26.509 --> 00:57:29.379
and I worked with Stephen
Miller, Pat Cipollone,

00:57:29.379 --> 00:57:32.382
Chad Wolf, Mark Morgan,
a whole group of people,

00:57:32.382 --> 00:57:34.984
Russ Volt, to figure out
how do we get the money,

00:57:34.984 --> 00:57:37.187
and then how do we get
it implemented and built?

00:57:37.187 --> 00:57:39.088
And so even on immigration.

00:57:39.088 --> 00:57:40.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think the merit-based
immigration system

00:57:40.957 --> 00:57:42.892 align:start size:97% position:3%
he was working on is something
that would have kept wages

00:57:42.892 --> 00:57:45.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
in America rising, brought
a lot of GDP creators

00:57:45.195 --> 00:57:46.463
to our country.

00:57:46.463 --> 00:57:47.997
People said he was
against immigrants,

00:57:47.997 --> 00:57:50.934
but he was wanting people to
come to our country legally

00:57:50.934 --> 00:57:52.735
and make America
a vacuum cleaner

00:57:52.735 --> 00:57:54.938 align:start size:88% position:13%
of all the best and
brightest in this world

00:57:54.938 --> 00:57:57.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
so that they will continue
to take our country

00:57:57.507 --> 00:57:58.975
to the next level.

00:57:58.975 --> 00:58:00.643
So I thought his policies
were really excellent.

00:58:00.643 --> 00:58:03.313 align:start size:84% position:16%
And to your question
specifically,

00:58:03.313 --> 00:58:06.282
I do give examples in the
book of Kim Kardashian

00:58:06.282 --> 00:58:10.253 align:start size:97% position:3%
or Van Jones, people who were
not naturally with Trump,

00:58:10.253 --> 00:58:12.956
coming to the White House on
an issue they cared about.

00:58:12.956 --> 00:58:14.190
Both of them took a lot of grief

00:58:14.190 --> 00:58:15.859
from people in the
liberal circles.

00:58:15.859 --> 00:58:17.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think Van was getting
called an Uncle Tom

00:58:17.594 --> 00:58:19.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
for coming and
working with Trump.

00:58:19.963 --> 00:58:21.631
But Van did something
that they didn't do.

00:58:21.631 --> 00:58:23.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, he had John Lewis
criticizing him for doing it,

00:58:23.600 --> 00:58:26.236 align:start position:0%
but meanwhile we got the
biggest criminal justice reform

00:58:26.236 --> 00:58:29.672 align:start size:97% position:3%
and prison reform bill done
in the history of our country.

00:58:29.672 --> 00:58:32.408
And so I think that
Trump was always,

00:58:32.408 --> 00:58:34.344
people who would go out and
criticize him on television,

00:58:34.344 --> 00:58:35.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
I always saw that
as virtue signaling,

00:58:35.845 --> 00:58:38.181
and I have no patience
for virtue signaling.

00:58:38.181 --> 00:58:40.483
But people who wanted
to get things done,

00:58:40.483 --> 00:58:41.784 align:start size:81% position:19%
a lot of people
would work with me

00:58:41.784 --> 00:58:43.019
behind the scenes on things.

00:58:43.019 --> 00:58:44.821
Some things didn't get
done in those meetings.

00:58:44.821 --> 00:58:47.023
You'd be surprised, some
people are in my office

00:58:47.023 --> 00:58:49.225
in the White House
meeting on policies.

00:58:49.225 --> 00:58:52.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Trump, any time
somebody wanted to reach out

00:58:52.061 --> 00:58:53.696
and meet with him,
he'd hear them out.

00:58:53.696 --> 00:58:55.331
Sometimes he would
agree, sometimes not.

00:58:55.331 --> 00:58:57.600
But I always saw him
more as a pragmatist

00:58:57.600 --> 00:58:59.035
than anything else.

00:58:59.035 --> 00:59:00.637
- I think I legally
have to ask you,

00:59:00.637 --> 00:59:03.373 align:start size:84% position:16%
so if, let's just say,
he was to run again

00:59:03.373 --> 00:59:06.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you get the call again,
or Ivanka gets the call

00:59:06.643 --> 00:59:09.846
or it's on speaker phone,
how are you gonna feel?

00:59:11.247 --> 00:59:15.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Look, I think that, and
I'll tell you what I believe.

00:59:15.218 --> 00:59:17.954
So number one with Trump, you
never know what he's gonna do,

00:59:17.954 --> 00:59:19.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you never know what's
gonna happen, right?

00:59:19.455 --> 00:59:22.759
That's the only thing that
you can say with certainty.

00:59:22.759 --> 00:59:27.764
Number two is I do believe
that he is very bothered

00:59:28.932 --> 00:59:30.600
by what he sees them
doing to this country.

00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:33.102
If you think about when
he left we had peace

00:59:33.102 --> 00:59:36.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
throughout the world, China
was on their back heel,

00:59:36.472 --> 00:59:37.874
we even had a deal
with the Taliban

00:59:37.874 --> 00:59:40.476
that I think would have
worked out really well,

00:59:40.476 --> 00:59:44.280
no wars in Europe, economy
was getting ready to explode,

00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:47.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
gas prices were low, inflation
was low, wages were rising.

00:59:47.216 --> 00:59:48.451
- It was pretty good.

00:59:48.451 --> 00:59:49.385
People forget, but
it was pretty good.

00:59:49.385 --> 00:59:51.020
- 2019 was the first year in 20

00:59:51.020 --> 00:59:53.756
that the wealth gap in our
country started to shrink,

00:59:53.756 --> 00:59:54.991
which was amazing,
and that was because

00:59:54.991 --> 00:59:56.693
people on the bottom
were making a lot more

00:59:56.693 --> 00:59:57.727
because of Trumponomics.

00:59:57.727 --> 01:00:00.597
So the policies were great.

01:00:00.597 --> 01:00:04.033
But now these guys come
in, and number one is

01:00:04.033 --> 01:00:06.536
the only thing that unites
them is that they hate Trump.

01:00:06.536 --> 01:00:08.771
That's why they're raiding
him and investigating him,

01:00:08.771 --> 01:00:10.206
and they can't stop
talking about him.

01:00:10.206 --> 01:00:12.609
And Biden just gave a
speech all about him,

01:00:12.609 --> 01:00:14.510
which is crazy after
he raids his home.

01:00:14.510 --> 01:00:16.579 align:start size:81% position:19%
Again, this is
getting very close

01:00:16.579 --> 01:00:18.281
to third world country stuff.

01:00:18.281 --> 01:00:20.917
This is stuff that we would
be mad at a dictator for.

01:00:22.218 --> 01:00:23.886
But his policies
were really good,

01:00:23.886 --> 01:00:27.323 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I wished when they came
in that they would say,

01:00:27.323 --> 01:00:29.092
do you know what, let's
just maybe talk the way

01:00:29.092 --> 01:00:30.426
politicians are
supposed to talk,

01:00:30.426 --> 01:00:32.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just keep all
of Trump's policies,

01:00:32.095 --> 01:00:33.763 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think they
could have gone down

01:00:33.763 --> 01:00:35.264
as an incredible administration.

01:00:35.264 --> 01:00:37.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
But instead they've
chosen to do the opposite,

01:00:37.734 --> 01:00:41.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think for him, he knows
that he could fix things

01:00:41.404 --> 01:00:44.440
really quickly, and that's
what I think is pushing him

01:00:44.440 --> 01:00:46.609
to want to do something, and
whether he makes a decision,

01:00:46.609 --> 01:00:48.277
that'll be up to him.

01:00:48.277 --> 01:00:50.413
But we'll see what
happens with that.

01:00:50.413 --> 01:00:52.815 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So let's finish up
with something that's
not Trump related

01:00:52.815 --> 01:00:55.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
and not directly DC
related or anything else,

01:00:55.284 --> 01:00:57.053
but when I moved
down to Florida,

01:00:57.053 --> 01:01:00.523 align:start position:0%
when we moved down in December,
I get an email from you,

01:01:00.523 --> 01:01:03.493 align:start position:0%
and it basically says, hey,
heard you're down here in Miami.

01:01:03.493 --> 01:01:05.028
Let's get together.

01:01:05.028 --> 01:01:07.263
Now we've become friendly and
had dinner a couple of times,

01:01:07.263 --> 01:01:09.632
and you said something to me
the first time that we met

01:01:09.632 --> 01:01:11.701 align:start size:81% position:19%
that has really
rung true with me,

01:01:11.701 --> 01:01:13.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is you said
something to the effect,

01:01:13.536 --> 01:01:15.238
and maybe you could, I'll
slightly butcher the quote

01:01:15.238 --> 01:01:17.640
and then you can fix it, but I
think you said something like

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:21.811
if America is to succeed,
it starts here in Miami,

01:01:21.811 --> 01:01:24.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
something to that effect, that
something is happening here

01:01:24.647 --> 01:01:26.649
in Miami, and more
broadly, Florida,

01:01:26.649 --> 01:01:29.719
that is the map for the
rest of the country.

01:01:29.719 --> 01:01:30.920
Now, clearly I believe that.

01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:32.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know how much
we're loving it here.

01:01:32.121 --> 01:01:34.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just opened these
offices and this studio.

01:01:34.724 --> 01:01:36.859
It's so flourishing here.

01:01:36.859 --> 01:01:39.929
What should people know about
what's going on down here?

01:01:39.929 --> 01:01:41.931
And did you guys always think
you were gonna come down here

01:01:41.931 --> 01:01:42.799
once DC was done?

01:01:42.799 --> 01:01:44.067
Was it immediate?

01:01:44.067 --> 01:01:45.268
Let's go down to
Florida and Miami?

01:01:45.268 --> 01:01:47.603
How did you even end up here?

01:01:47.603 --> 01:01:49.605
- It was very last minute.

01:01:49.605 --> 01:01:52.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
First of all, I was very
glad when I saw you move down

01:01:52.175 --> 01:01:53.242
because you were a
voice that I followed

01:01:53.242 --> 01:01:54.510
when I was in the White House,

01:01:54.510 --> 01:01:56.145
and I know we'd
missed each other

01:01:56.145 --> 01:01:58.314
at one of the parties
in the White House.

01:01:58.314 --> 01:02:00.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when I saw you come
down, I said this is great,

01:02:00.583 --> 01:02:03.419 align:start size:84% position:16%
because Florida
people are forming,

01:02:03.419 --> 01:02:05.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a place where
everyone's helping everyone.

01:02:05.488 --> 01:02:08.124
And so the decision
was like this, right.

01:02:08.124 --> 01:02:10.426
We're figuring out
what we want to do.

01:02:10.426 --> 01:02:12.395
We traveled the whole
country, so we'd seen

01:02:12.395 --> 01:02:13.596
all these different places.

01:02:13.596 --> 01:02:15.465 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we'd identified maybe
two or three cities

01:02:15.465 --> 01:02:18.701 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we thought could be
really exciting places to go.

01:02:18.701 --> 01:02:20.403
New York was out of the
question at the time

01:02:20.403 --> 01:02:22.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it was
basically like Pompei.

01:02:22.605 --> 01:02:25.508
It was closed down, the
restaurants were closed,

01:02:25.508 --> 01:02:28.010
the school were closed, none
of our friends were there.

01:02:28.010 --> 01:02:30.813
Everyone had either gone
to the Hamptons or Florida,

01:02:30.813 --> 01:02:32.949
or some people living in Aspen.

01:02:32.949 --> 01:02:35.151
And we basically said, you know,

01:02:35.151 --> 01:02:37.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
this doesn't feel
like the place to go.

01:02:37.386 --> 01:02:40.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we said, Florida
we honed in on.

01:02:40.923 --> 01:02:42.358
I joked with Ivanka.

01:02:42.358 --> 01:02:43.826 align:start position:0%
I said, look, I know we're
about 30 years ahead of schedule

01:02:43.826 --> 01:02:45.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
because most old people
end up here anyway.

01:02:45.928 --> 01:02:46.896
(Dave speaks in
foreign language)

01:02:46.896 --> 01:02:48.431
Exactly.

01:02:48.431 --> 01:02:52.268
But it felt like a place that
had the energy of the future.

01:02:52.268 --> 01:02:53.770
And so we come down
here, and we said,

01:02:53.770 --> 01:02:55.438
look, if we don't like it,
we'll put the kids in school,

01:02:55.438 --> 01:02:57.540
it has a very nice
Jewish community,

01:02:57.540 --> 01:03:00.777
then we'll go back to
New York in the fall.

01:03:00.777 --> 01:03:02.311
We came down and we
just fell in love.

01:03:02.311 --> 01:03:04.413
I mean, first of all,
the weather's great,

01:03:04.413 --> 01:03:05.915
the energy's great.

01:03:05.915 --> 01:03:08.317
What really surprised us,
though, was the people.

01:03:08.317 --> 01:03:12.455 align:start size:97% position:3%
So right now you have people
from LA, San Francisco,

01:03:12.455 --> 01:03:14.690
Chicago, New York coming down.

01:03:14.690 --> 01:03:16.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
But you have an original
crop of Miamians

01:03:16.058 --> 01:03:17.960
who are also phenomenal.

01:03:17.960 --> 01:03:18.895
- [Dave] Yeah.

01:03:18.895 --> 01:03:20.096
- Everyone's coming together.

01:03:20.096 --> 01:03:21.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
When we were coming
up in New York,

01:03:21.931 --> 01:03:24.033 align:start size:84% position:16%
it felt like a city
that had become great.

01:03:24.033 --> 01:03:26.836
Over the previous decades,
people had fought to make it.

01:03:26.836 --> 01:03:28.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
Here you have people
coming together

01:03:28.538 --> 01:03:30.039
trying to create a great city.

01:03:30.039 --> 01:03:32.909 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're all very
invested in doing things

01:03:32.909 --> 01:03:34.343
civically, socially.

01:03:34.343 --> 01:03:36.479
We've made a ton of
amazing new friends.

01:03:36.479 --> 01:03:39.515
I've call it sometimes it's
like freshman year of college

01:03:39.515 --> 01:03:42.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
where everyone's creating
new friend groups,

01:03:42.418 --> 01:03:43.953
trying new hobbies.

01:03:43.953 --> 01:03:48.291
When you go to, with your
kids, you have activities,

01:03:48.291 --> 01:03:50.560
taking them fishing,
taking them boating.

01:03:50.560 --> 01:03:52.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's just a very
creative place.

01:03:52.628 --> 01:03:54.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, what I find is
everyone comes through.

01:03:54.130 --> 01:03:57.433
So what I loved in Washington
was that the local community

01:03:57.433 --> 01:04:00.169
were like jackals because
it's a one industry town,

01:04:00.169 --> 01:04:03.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
but you always had
very interesting people

01:04:03.406 --> 01:04:08.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
from business, from
intellectual, just
passing through

01:04:08.010 --> 01:04:09.946
trying to influence in one way.

01:04:09.946 --> 01:04:11.848
Here you have an
amazing community,

01:04:11.848 --> 01:04:13.583
but you also have a lot
of people coming through.

01:04:13.583 --> 01:04:16.619
And then what I find is that
it's a very tolerant place,

01:04:16.619 --> 01:04:18.054
and that you can be
at dinner with people

01:04:18.054 --> 01:04:20.389
very liberal, very conservative.

01:04:20.389 --> 01:04:21.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
Everyone wants to
learn from each other,

01:04:21.958 --> 01:04:24.427
and I find that it's
a place where people

01:04:24.427 --> 01:04:26.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
don't take themselves
too seriously.

01:04:26.495 --> 01:04:27.997
Everyone's doing
interesting things,

01:04:27.997 --> 01:04:29.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
everyone has
interesting stories,

01:04:29.398 --> 01:04:31.067
and the lifestyle's amazing.

01:04:31.067 --> 01:04:33.236
So that's been our
experience so far.

01:04:33.236 --> 01:04:36.205 align:start size:88% position:13%
I know that you're
also converted as well.

01:04:36.205 --> 01:04:38.307
- There is nothing that I
can possibly say right now

01:04:38.307 --> 01:04:41.711
that my audience has not
heard me say about Florida.

01:04:41.711 --> 01:04:43.145
- Did I repeat a
lot of your talk?

01:04:43.145 --> 01:04:45.915 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It was pretty much, yeah,
it's pretty good here.

01:04:45.915 --> 01:04:47.216
That's the point.

01:04:47.216 --> 01:04:49.151
And it must feel kind of
refreshing for you guys

01:04:49.151 --> 01:04:50.720
in that regard, that
you're sort of out

01:04:50.720 --> 01:04:52.788 align:start position:0%
of the political thing, and
then you get to build something

01:04:52.788 --> 01:04:55.524
in a place that seems to
represent some of the ideas

01:04:55.524 --> 01:04:56.993
you were trying to get
across to the country.

01:04:56.993 --> 01:04:58.494
- Yeah, we don't miss it.

01:04:58.494 --> 01:05:01.163
And I will say too,
Florida, and again,

01:05:01.163 --> 01:05:02.531
it's been interesting
that it's becoming

01:05:02.531 --> 01:05:06.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
this big Republican
epicenter now,

01:05:06.202 --> 01:05:07.703
the free state of Florida.

01:05:07.703 --> 01:05:10.673
But it feels like it's more of
a freedom driven Republican,

01:05:10.673 --> 01:05:12.275
a no judgment.

01:05:12.275 --> 01:05:14.477
I think a lot of the
future of what I think

01:05:14.477 --> 01:05:16.512
conservatism can be.

01:05:16.512 --> 01:05:17.747
And again, the one
thing I'll say too,

01:05:17.747 --> 01:05:19.548
I'm very hesitant to
use labels, right?

01:05:19.548 --> 01:05:22.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
I learned that we're
basically a two party country

01:05:22.385 --> 01:05:24.620 align:start size:88% position:13%
right now, but Democrats
and Republicans,

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:26.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're each a
collection of tribes

01:05:26.589 --> 01:05:28.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
more than they're like
ubiquitous parties.

01:05:28.257 --> 01:05:30.526
But I feel like here in Florida,

01:05:30.526 --> 01:05:33.796
it's just a lot of people
doing creative things,

01:05:33.796 --> 01:05:35.431
and a lot of fun people.

01:05:35.431 --> 01:05:38.334
So it's been a lot of fun
to be a part of it so far.

01:05:38.334 --> 01:05:40.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, thank you for coming
down to our Locals studio

01:05:40.703 --> 01:05:41.938
here in Miami.

01:05:41.938 --> 01:05:44.941
You are the first guest
in the Locals studio.

01:05:44.941 --> 01:05:47.910
- You were actually the first
guest in the Locals studio.

01:05:47.910 --> 01:05:50.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Technically I was the
first guest in my own studio.

01:05:50.179 --> 01:05:51.347
- Yeah, I watched that one.

01:05:51.347 --> 01:05:52.281
- Good to see you, my man.

01:05:52.281 --> 01:05:53.482
- Thank you, Dave.

01:05:53.482 --> 01:05:54.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
